export const economicsDepth = {
  "topicFrames": {
    "Adam Smith & Classical Economics": "Classical political economy treated growth and distribution as dynamic problems: how specialization raises productivity, how capital accumulation changes wages and profits, and how institutions shape exchange. Smith's system is broader than a simple claim that self-interest automatically produces good outcomes. Competitive markets can coordinate decentralized activity, but the result depends on justice, enforceable contracts, mobility, competition and public institutions. Classical writers also reasoned in terms of social classes and factor incomes: wages, profits and rents. Their value theories preceded the later marginalist framework, so it is useful to distinguish their historical analytical language from modern supply-and-demand models. The enduring contribution is the attempt to explain an economy as an evolving system in which productivity, market size, institutions and distribution interact over time.",
    "Schools & Thinkers": "History of economic thought is most useful when treated as a sequence of competing models rather than a list of doctrines. Each school selects different state variables, frictions and adjustment mechanisms: classical economists emphasized production and distribution; marginalists individual choice and relative scarcity; neoclassical theory equilibrium and optimization; public-choice theory incentives inside government; supply-side approaches tax and regulatory effects on productive capacity; development economics structural transformation and institutions. Apparent disagreements often come from different time horizons or assumptions. A policy can raise demand in the short run while changing incentives or capacity in the long run. When comparing schools, ask what is held fixed, what can adjust, how expectations are formed, whether markets clear, and what evidence would distinguish one mechanism from another. That analytical discipline matters more than memorizing labels.",
    "Marx & Marxian Economics": "Marxian economics analyzes capitalism as a historically specific system of production organized around wage labor, private control of productive assets and competitive accumulation. Its central objects are not only prices and quantities but social relations: who owns productive assets, how surplus is generated and appropriated, how competition disciplines firms, and how accumulation restructures technology and class relations. Marx's value categories are not identical to modern national-accounting or marginal-product concepts, so translations should be made carefully. Modern Marxian work includes multiple interpretations of value, profits, crisis and class, and it overlaps with economic history, political economy and sociology. The framework is strongest when used to analyze accumulation, bargaining power, institutional conflict and structural change; empirical claims about profit rates, exploitation or crisis mechanisms require separate testing rather than being assumed from the vocabulary alone.",
    "Keynesian Economics": "The Keynesian core is a theory of coordination failure in a monetary economy. Output is not assumed to sit continuously at a supply-determined full-employment level: firms produce in response to expected sales, investment depends on financing conditions and uncertain future returns, and wages and prices adjust imperfectly. Modern Keynesian models formalize these ideas through nominal rigidities, imperfect competition, expectations and intertemporal decisions. The policy question is therefore state-dependent. Fiscal or monetary expansion can have large real effects when resources are idle and policy rates or credit channels constrain private spending, but smaller or inflationary effects near capacity. Expectations, debt sustainability, exchange-rate regimes and central-bank reaction functions all matter. Keynesian analysis is consequently less about a permanent preference for intervention than about identifying when aggregate demand, financial conditions and nominal frictions prevent rapid self-correction.",
    "Friedman & Monetarism": "Monetarism shifted macroeconomic attention toward nominal aggregates, expectations and the limits of discretionary stabilization. In the classic quantity-theory tradition, sustained inflation ultimately requires sustained nominal spending growth relative to real productive capacity. Friedman also stressed that households smooth consumption using longer-horizon income expectations and that attempts to hold unemployment below its equilibrium level would be undermined by changing inflation expectations. The historical monetarist policy prescription of controlling money growth became harder to implement when financial innovation made money demand unstable and central banks moved toward interest-rate targeting. Even so, its deeper lessons survive in modern macroeconomics: policy changes alter expectations, nominal variables cannot permanently substitute for real constraints, and central-bank credibility affects inflation dynamics. The important distinction is between the old operational rule of fixed money growth and the broader monetary theory of nominal stabilization.",
    "Hayek & Austrian Economics": "Austrian economics treats markets primarily as processes of coordination and discovery rather than static solutions to known optimization problems. Knowledge about preferences, local conditions, technologies and opportunities is dispersed and often tacit; prices and profit-and-loss signals help agents adjust without any participant possessing the full system. Hayek's argument is therefore institutional and epistemic as much as allocative. Mises added a calculation argument about capital goods when market prices are absent, while later Austrians emphasized entrepreneurship and disequilibrium discovery. The school is more skeptical than mainstream economics of aggregate models and formal equilibrium analysis. Its strongest contribution is the focus on information, institutions and entrepreneurial adaptation. Its more controversial claims, especially Austrian business-cycle theory, depend on specific assumptions about credit, capital structure and intertemporal coordination that are not accepted as a general explanation of modern cycles.",
    "Institutional Economics": "Institutional economics asks why identical technologies and resources can generate different outcomes under different rules. Property rights, contract enforcement, political constraints, organizational forms, norms and state capacity change expected returns and transaction costs. New institutional economics often begins with incomplete contracts and costly exchange: if every contingency cannot be specified or enforced, governance structures matter. Douglass North added historical persistence and path dependence, while later political-economy work examines how institutions distribute power and create constituencies that defend existing rules. Causality is difficult because institutions are endogenous to income, conflict and history; prosperous societies can build better institutions just as better institutions can support prosperity. Strong analysis therefore distinguishes a plausible mechanism from a clean causal estimate and avoids reducing institutional quality to a single index or slogan.",
    "Behavioral Economics": "Behavioral economics modifies the standard model where evidence shows systematic departures from stable, fully informed, time-consistent optimization. The aim is not to declare people irrational but to specify alternative mechanisms: reference dependence, loss aversion, limited attention, probability weighting, social preferences or present bias. A useful behavioral model must generate predictions that differ from the benchmark and survive changes in incentives, experience and context. Welfare analysis is harder because observed choices may not reveal stable preferences when framing or self-control problems matter. That creates a distinction between predicting behavior and deciding which behavior reflects welfare. Modern work therefore combines experiments, field data and structural models, and often studies how institutions can either exploit or mitigate behavioral biases. Heterogeneity is central: an average treatment effect can conceal large differences across people and settings.",
    "Schumpeter & Innovation": "Schumpeterian economics shifts attention from static efficiency to dynamic competition. Firms do not merely choose quantities given existing technologies; they invest, experiment and introduce new products, processes and organizational forms. Successful innovation can generate temporary monopoly rents that finance and reward risky discovery, while entry and imitation erode those rents. This creates a tension in competition policy: strong current competition can discipline incumbents, but some expected market power may be necessary to justify costly innovation. Productivity effects also depend on diffusion and complementary investment, so major technologies can take years to appear in aggregate productivity data. The framework is especially useful for understanding industry turnover, venture finance, technological waves and why economic growth involves both creation and destruction rather than smooth movement along a fixed production function.",
    "Microeconomics": "Microeconomics studies choices and market outcomes under constraints. The central analytical tools are optimization, marginal conditions, elasticities and equilibrium. A competitive equilibrium result is never just a diagram: it follows from assumptions about preferences, technology, information, property rights and market structure. Comparative statics asks how an equilibrium changes when one parameter changes, while welfare economics asks whether gains to some agents exceed losses to others and under what institutional assumptions. Many apparent paradoxes disappear once incidence is separated from legal assignment, average effects from marginal effects, and short-run elasticities from long-run responses. Modern microeconomics extends far beyond perfect competition to strategic interaction, information asymmetry, contracting, networks and market design. The discipline is strongest when the model is explicit about who chooses what, what constraints bind, and what margin can actually adjust.",
    "Macroeconomics": "Macroeconomics links aggregate expenditure, production, inflation, employment and financial conditions across time. Accounting identities such as Y = C + I + G + NX always hold ex post, but they do not by themselves establish causal mechanisms. The analytical problem is to explain how households, firms, banks, governments and central banks jointly determine real activity and nominal variables. Short-run models emphasize demand, nominal rigidities and financial frictions; long-run growth models emphasize capital, labor, productivity and institutions. Expectations connect the two because current spending and pricing depend on beliefs about future income, inflation and policy. Identification is difficult: recessions change fiscal balances and interest rates even without policy action, so correlations can reverse causality. Serious macro analysis therefore separates identities, behavioral equations, policy rules and exogenous shocks.",
    "Money, Banking & Finance": "Modern finance is built on intertemporal prices, balance sheets and risk. Interest rates convert future cash flows into present values; risk premia compensate for exposure to states in which marginal wealth is especially valuable; leverage amplifies both returns and losses. Banks are not passive intermediaries that simply relend a fixed stock of deposits. Lending simultaneously creates an asset and a deposit, while capital, liquidity, credit risk, funding conditions and regulation constrain expansion. Central banks influence the price and availability of liquidity through policy rates, reserves, asset purchases and lender-of-last-resort facilities, but transmission depends on banks, markets and expectations. Financial stability problems often arise from maturity transformation: institutions fund long-duration or illiquid assets with liabilities that can run. Understanding who holds which asset and liability is often more informative than tracking a single aggregate.",
    "Trade & Global Economy": "International economics separates real trade mechanisms from financial-account identities. Comparative advantage concerns relative opportunity costs, not absolute productivity, and implies potential gains from specialization even when one country is more productive in every sector. Those aggregate gains do not imply that every worker, region or firm benefits; distribution and adjustment costs can be large and persistent. Exchange rates connect trade to monetary and financial conditions, while the balance of payments ensures that current-account positions correspond to financial flows. Open economies also face policy constraints: with high capital mobility, a country cannot simultaneously maintain a fixed exchange rate and an independent monetary policy. Modern trade occurs through global value chains, so tariffs and exchange-rate movements affect imported intermediate inputs as well as final goods. Incidence therefore depends on elasticities, invoicing, market power and supply-chain structure.",
    "Development & Institutions": "Development economics studies sustained increases in productive capacity, income and human welfare rather than one-time growth. Poor economies often face interacting constraints: low state capacity, weak infrastructure, limited human capital, shallow finance, informality and sectoral misallocation. Structural transformation moves labor and capital from low-productivity activities toward higher-productivity firms and sectors, but that process requires urbanization, skills, logistics and institutions. Cross-country correlations are informative but fragile because geography, history and institutions co-evolve. Micro-level randomized trials can establish causal effects of specific interventions, yet scaling them may change prices, institutions or political incentives. Macro development therefore requires combining credible local evidence with general-equilibrium reasoning. The key question is not simply whether a policy works somewhere, but which constraint it relaxes, whether that constraint is binding, and how the surrounding system responds.",
    "Inequality, Labor & Political Economy": "Labor and political economy combine market forces with institutions and bargaining. Wages reflect productivity, scarcity and outside options, but also search frictions, employer market power, collective bargaining and regulation. This makes labor markets different from frictionless spot markets: workers incur switching costs, firms possess wage-setting power, and employment relationships are repeated. Distributional analysis must separate flows such as income from stocks such as wealth, and distinguish pre-tax market outcomes from taxes and transfers. Political-economy models add collective action, rent seeking and institutional incentives. Policies create winners and losers, and groups with concentrated stakes often organize more effectively than diffuse publics. These models do not imply that every regulation is captured or every redistribution inefficient; they provide mechanisms for analyzing how economic and political incentives interact.",
    "Crises & Economic History": "Economic crises are best understood through balance sheets, policy regimes and institutional constraints rather than as isolated dates. Financial booms often combine leverage, maturity mismatch, optimistic expectations and rising asset prices; the downturn becomes severe when falling collateral values force deleveraging and credit contraction. Monetary regimes matter because a gold standard, fixed exchange rate or currency union can limit domestic stabilization options. Banking panics can convert solvency concerns into liquidity crises, while fiscal stress can feed back into bank balance sheets. Historical episodes are not interchangeable: the Great Depression, Asian crisis, global financial crisis and euro crisis had different triggers and institutional structures. Comparative history is valuable precisely because it shows which mechanisms recur and which depend on a specific monetary, regulatory or political architecture.",
    "Behavioral & Information Economics": "Information economics studies markets in which agents know different things or cannot perfectly observe one another's actions. Adverse selection occurs before contracting when hidden types affect participation; moral hazard occurs after contracting when hidden actions change risk. Signaling and screening are strategic responses, but they consume resources and can separate types imperfectly. Behavioral mechanisms add limits to attention, belief formation and intertemporal control. Information cascades show how rational individuals can ignore private signals when public behavior is sufficiently informative, while winner's-curse effects arise when the highest bidder is systematically the one with the most optimistic estimate. Market design uses these insights constructively by changing rules, timing and information to improve matching or allocation. The common theme is that institutional details matter because information and incentives are part of the market itself, not external complications."
  },
  "notes": {
    "econ-classical": "The classical tradition is not a single model. Smith, Ricardo, Malthus and Mill disagreed about value, distribution and long-run growth, but they shared an interest in how accumulation changes the shares of income going to labor, capital and land. Ricardo's rent theory, for example, makes distribution endogenous to scarcity of high-quality land; his trade theory separates relative from absolute productivity. Classical analysis also tends to be dynamic: capital accumulation can raise output while changing wages, profits and population. Reading it through modern marginalist language is useful, but it can hide the original focus on social classes, reproduction of the economic system and historical development.",
    "econ-adam-smith": "Smith's analytical system combines increasing returns from specialization with a theory of market coordination and a theory of institutions. His famous invisible-hand passage is a narrow part of a much broader argument. He worried about monopoly, collusion, worker deskilling and the political influence of merchants, and he assigned government roles in defense, justice, education and public works. The deeper mechanism is that specialization raises productivity, but specialization itself is limited by market size; larger markets make finer specialization profitable, which can create cumulative growth. Smith also distinguished market prices from longer-run natural prices and treated competition as a process that moves capital across uses.",
    "econ-division-labor": "Division of labor raises productivity through learning-by-doing, reduced setup time, specialized tools and the ability to match heterogeneous skills to tasks. But specialization also creates coordination costs and vulnerability to bottlenecks. In modern production networks the relevant question is therefore not maximal specialization but the optimal boundary between specialization and redundancy. Smith also linked specialization to market size: a narrow market cannot support highly specialized occupations because there is insufficient demand. That idea reappears in modern increasing-returns and economic-geography models, where larger markets can sustain more varieties and deeper supplier networks. At the firm level, specialization can increase output while reducing worker task variety, creating a separate human-capital and organizational tradeoff.",
    "econ-ricardo": "Ricardo's most durable contribution is comparative advantage, but his system was primarily about distribution. He analyzed how wages, profits and rents evolve as capital accumulates and cultivation extends to less productive land. Diminishing returns in agriculture could raise rents and food costs, compressing profits and slowing accumulation. Comparative advantage fits this broader logic because trade can relax domestic scarcity by allowing countries to specialize according to relative costs. The theorem does not require balanced bilateral trade, equal wages or identical technologies. What matters is opportunity cost. Modern trade theory adds factor mobility, scale economies, firm heterogeneity and adjustment costs, but Ricardo's core insight survives: absolute productivity rankings do not determine the direction of mutually beneficial specialization.",
    "econ-malthus": "Malthus's population principle assumed that population could respond positively to income while food production faced tighter physical limits, creating a tendency for living standards to return toward subsistence. In preindustrial economies this mechanism has some empirical relevance and is related to the Malthusian model used in economic history. The industrial transition weakened the prediction because technological progress accelerated and demographic behavior changed: fertility eventually fell as income, urbanization and education rose. Malthus also wrote on insufficient demand and gluts, anticipating later debates about whether aggregate saving automatically translates into investment. The important analytical lesson is that population dynamics, technology and resource constraints are jointly determined; extrapolating any one trend while holding the others fixed can be misleading.",
    "econ-marxian": "Marxian economics treats profit and accumulation as embedded in class relations rather than as purely technological returns to capital. Capitalists compete, reinvest and introduce labor-saving technologies, while workers sell labor power because productive assets are unequally owned. The system is therefore dynamic and conflictual even when individual firms behave competitively. Different Marxian traditions disagree on how to interpret the labor theory of value, the transformation from values to prices, the tendency of the profit rate and the sources of crisis. Contemporary use is strongest when it specifies a mechanism such as bargaining power, concentration, financial fragility or technological displacement rather than treating capitalism as having a single inevitable trajectory.",
    "econ-surplus-value": "In Marx's framework, labor power is purchased as a commodity whose value is related to the cost of reproducing the worker, while the labor performed during production can create a larger value. The difference is surplus value. Absolute surplus value can rise by extending or intensifying work; relative surplus value can rise when productivity lowers the value of wage goods and reduces the labor time needed to reproduce labor power. This is not the same concept as accounting profit or modern economic surplus, because Marx's categories are embedded in his theory of value. A careful comparison with mainstream economics therefore requires translating the underlying assumptions rather than equating surplus value directly with observed profit margins.",
    "econ-marginalism": "The marginal revolution replaced much classical value reasoning with an analysis of incremental tradeoffs. Jevons, Menger and Walras developed different versions, but the common move was to explain value using marginal utility, scarcity and choice rather than embodied labor alone. This makes equilibrium prices jointly determined by preferences and production conditions. Marginalism also changed the mathematical form of economics: optimum conditions could be written as equalities between marginal benefit and marginal cost, and general equilibrium could study simultaneous market clearing. The shift did not make historical or institutional questions irrelevant, but it created the analytical language that later became standard in consumer theory, firm theory and welfare economics.",
    "econ-neoclassical": "Neoclassical economics is best understood as a family of models built around constrained optimization and equilibrium, not a single ideological position. Consumers maximize objectives subject to budgets; firms choose inputs and outputs given technology and prices; equilibrium coordinates those decisions. Powerful welfare results follow under restrictive assumptions such as complete markets, perfect information and price-taking behavior. Once those assumptions fail, market outcomes need not be efficient. Modern mainstream economics therefore contains models of monopoly, asymmetric information, externalities, search frictions and behavioral deviations within broadly neoclassical mathematical methods. The important distinction is between the optimization toolkit and the stronger claim that real markets approximate the conditions under which competitive equilibria have desirable welfare properties.",
    "econ-marshall": "Marshall's partial-equilibrium method isolates one market while treating other prices and incomes as approximately fixed. That simplification made supply-and-demand analysis operational, but Marshall was careful about time horizons. Market-period supply can be nearly fixed, short-run supply depends on variable inputs, and long-run supply allows capacity and firm entry to adjust. His elasticity concept formalized responsiveness rather than mere direction of change. Marshall also analyzed consumer surplus and increasing returns, and he recognized that industry-level scale economies could complicate simple competitive equilibrium. The ceteris-paribus method remains central: it gives clean causal intuition, but the analyst must know when feedback through other markets is too large for partial equilibrium to be reliable.",
    "econ-keynesian": "The crucial Keynesian departure is that planned saving and planned investment need not be coordinated by an interest-rate adjustment quickly enough to maintain full employment. If households cut spending, firms experience weaker sales and may cut output and employment; realized saving can then fail to rise because aggregate income falls. Modern New Keynesian models translate this into intertemporal optimization with sticky prices and a monetary-policy rule, while Post-Keynesian approaches emphasize fundamental uncertainty, finance and historical time. The practical question is the slope of aggregate supply and the strength of policy transmission in a particular state. Demand stimulus in a depressed economy and the same stimulus at full capacity are not equivalent experiments.",
    "econ-keynes": "Keynes's General Theory challenged the presumption that flexible markets necessarily return the economy rapidly to full employment. Investment is forward-looking and depends on expectations that cannot always be reduced to known probabilities. If expected returns collapse, lower wages can worsen debt burdens and demand rather than restore employment. Keynes's liquidity preference theory also made money a portfolio choice: the interest rate is partly the price required to induce wealth holders to hold less liquid assets. Later Keynesians formalized some mechanisms differently, so not everything called Keynesian appears literally in Keynes. His enduring contribution is the idea that aggregate outcomes can be constrained by deficient spending and coordination even when no individual actor is making an obvious optimization error.",
    "econ-post-keynesian": "Post-Keynesian economics rejects the idea that uncertainty can always be represented as known probability distributions and places financial institutions at the center of macro dynamics. Investment drives output and income, while saving adjusts ex post; money and credit are largely endogenous to bank lending rather than fixed by a money multiplier. Many Post-Keynesians use stock-flow consistent models to ensure that every flow changes sectoral balance sheets coherently. Pricing may follow mark-up rules rather than continuous market clearing, and distribution between wages and profits can affect demand. The tradition is heterogeneous, but its distinctive contribution is to combine effective demand with balance-sheet dynamics, institutional detail and path dependence instead of treating the economy as returning automatically to a unique long-run equilibrium.",
    "econ-monetarism": "Classic monetarism can be written around MV = PY, but the equation is an identity; the theory comes from assumptions about the behavior of velocity, real output and money supply. If money demand is stable and real output is pinned down by real factors in the long run, persistent excess money growth appears mainly as inflation. Friedman also argued that policy operates with long and variable lags, making activist fine-tuning risky. The monetarist experiment of targeting monetary aggregates encountered unstable relationships as financial systems evolved. Modern central banks therefore target short-term interest rates, yet monetary conditions still matter through nominal spending, credit and expectations. The key lesson is not that one money aggregate mechanically controls prices, but that sustained nominal expansion cannot permanently outrun real capacity.",
    "econ-friedman": "Friedman's influence spans monetary theory, consumption, labor markets and policy methodology. The permanent-income hypothesis separates transitory income from expected long-run resources, explaining why consumption responds less than one-for-one to temporary income changes. His natural-rate argument added expectations to the Phillips curve: an unemployment-inflation tradeoff cannot be permanently exploited once workers and firms revise expected inflation. With Schwartz, he argued that monetary contraction and banking failures deepened the Great Depression, strengthening the case for central-bank stabilization during panics. Friedman favored simple rules partly because he distrusted policymakers' information and timing. His substantive legacy is therefore broader than a general preference for smaller government.",
    "econ-austrian": "Austrian economics differs from standard equilibrium analysis in its emphasis on market process. Prices are not merely equilibrium parameters; they are signals generated through entrepreneurial experimentation, error and revision. Capital is heterogeneous and time-structured, so changes in interest rates can alter the pattern of investment across stages of production. Mises grounded economics in purposive action, while Hayek focused on dispersed knowledge. This framework is especially illuminating for entrepreneurship and institutional comparison. Its business-cycle account is more controversial because it attributes unsustainable investment patterns to credit conditions that distort intertemporal price signals. Evaluating that claim requires evidence about credit allocation, capital structure and whether observed booms fit the specific mechanism rather than merely involving low rates.",
    "econ-hayek": "Hayek's knowledge problem is often misunderstood as a claim that governments know nothing. The argument is comparative: much economically relevant knowledge is local, changing and tacit, so institutions differ in how they aggregate and use it. Competitive prices compress information about relative scarcity without requiring agents to know the underlying causes. Hayek also emphasized that competition is a discovery procedure; if all relevant information were already known, much of competition's informational role would disappear. This does not imply that every price is efficient or that regulation is always harmful. Externalities, monopoly and information asymmetries remain real. The Hayekian question is what knowledge a proposed institution requires and whether participants have incentives to reveal or act on that knowledge.",
    "econ-mises": "Mises's calculation argument concerns capital allocation in an economy where the means of production are not traded. Without exchange prices for heterogeneous capital goods, a planner cannot easily compare alternative uses in a common monetary metric that reflects opportunity costs. The argument is stronger than saying computation is hard: it claims that some information is generated by market exchange itself. Critics respond that large organizations plan internally and that optimization methods can allocate resources under explicit objectives, while Austrians emphasize that real preferences, technologies and entrepreneurial discoveries are not fixed inputs. The modern relevance is therefore about institutional generation of information and incentives, not merely the computational capacity of a central computer.",
    "econ-institutional": "Institutional economics broadens the unit of analysis from isolated exchange to the rules that make exchange possible. A contract is valuable only if rights are defined, disputes can be resolved and counterparties expect enforcement. Informal norms can substitute for formal law in repeated communities, while formal law becomes more important as exchange expands beyond close networks. Institutions also shape distribution, so inefficient rules can persist when they benefit powerful groups. This creates a political-economy problem: changing a rule can increase aggregate output while threatening incumbents who can block reform. The strongest institutional explanations identify a specific rule, incentive and enforcement mechanism rather than using institutions as a residual explanation for every cross-country difference.",
    "econ-new-institutional": "New institutional economics applies price-theoretic reasoning to organizations and rules. Coase's transaction-cost insight asks why some exchanges occur through markets while others occur inside firms. Williamson developed governance structures around asset specificity, uncertainty and opportunism: when relationship-specific investments create hold-up risk, vertical integration or long-term contracts may dominate spot exchange. Property-rights theory focuses on residual control when contracts are incomplete. The approach does not assume firms are intrinsically more efficient than markets; each governance mode has costs. Its comparative method is to match transactions to institutions that economize on contracting and enforcement problems. That makes organizational boundaries endogenous rather than given.",
    "econ-behavioral": "Behavioral economics is most useful when a documented deviation changes equilibrium or policy conclusions. Loss aversion can make labor supply or asset trading reference-dependent; present bias can create demand for commitment devices; limited attention can reduce responses to taxes that are not salient. These mechanisms can be incorporated into formal models rather than treated as anecdotes. A central challenge is welfare: if revealed preference is unstable across frames, the analyst needs another criterion for identifying mistakes. Some interventions, such as defaults, can help inattentive agents but may also be chosen strategically by firms or governments. Behavioral economics therefore turns the design of choice architecture itself into an economic object.",
    "econ-public-choice": "Public choice applies incentive analysis to political actors rather than assuming government automatically implements a social optimum. Voters face information costs, officials pursue careers and budgets, and organized groups may invest in lobbying when policy creates concentrated rents. Collective decisions can therefore exhibit agency problems and rent seeking just as firms do. The framework does not prove that government failure exceeds market failure; it changes the benchmark. A serious comparison asks which institution performs better under realistic information and incentive constraints. Concepts such as rational ignorance, logrolling and concentrated benefits help explain why politically durable policies may not maximize aggregate surplus.",
    "econ-new-classical": "New classical macroeconomics introduced rational expectations and explicit microfoundations into macro models. Rational expectations does not mean people forecast perfectly; it means forecast errors are not systematically predictable using information embedded in the model. This undermined policy rules that rely on repeatedly surprising private agents. Lucas's critique goes further: estimated historical relationships can change when policy regimes change because people alter behavior. Early models also used market-clearing and imperfect-information mechanisms to explain cycles. Modern macroeconomics retained rational expectations and policy-invariance concerns even as New Keynesian models reintroduced nominal rigidities. The legacy is methodological: credible policy analysis must model how expectations and decision rules respond to the policy itself.",
    "econ-new-keynesian": "New Keynesian economics combines optimizing households and firms with nominal rigidities, usually generated by staggered price setting or adjustment costs. Because only some firms can reset prices each period, a change in nominal demand affects real output in the short run. A basic three-equation model links an intertemporal IS relation, a New Keynesian Phillips curve and a central-bank policy rule. Expectations are central: anticipated future policy affects current spending and pricing. The framework also explains why credible monetary policy can stabilize both inflation and the output gap under some shocks, while supply shocks create a tradeoff. Modern versions add financial frictions, heterogeneous households and labor-market search because the baseline representative-agent model misses important transmission channels.",
    "econ-supply-side": "Supply-side economics emphasizes policies that alter potential output by changing incentives to work, save, invest and innovate. The analytical object is the tax wedge between private and social returns. A tax-rate cut can raise taxable activity, but the revenue effect depends on the relevant elasticity and starting tax rate; the Laffer curve is a general relationship, not evidence that any particular cut pays for itself. Supply reforms also operate slowly because capital formation, labor-force participation and innovation adjust over time. Distinguishing level effects from growth-rate effects is essential: a reform can permanently raise the level of output without permanently raising its long-run growth rate. Distributional and fiscal consequences must be evaluated alongside efficiency effects.",
    "econ-development-school": "Development economics evolved from big-push and structuralist theories toward a combination of growth theory, institutional analysis and micro-level causal inference. The field studies coordination failures, credit constraints, human capital, state capacity and the movement of workers across sectors. A central issue is complementarity: a factory may not be profitable without roads, skills and suppliers, so individually rational decisions can generate a low-investment equilibrium. Modern work often tests narrow interventions with randomized or quasi-experimental methods, but macro development requires asking whether results survive scaling and general equilibrium. The most informative models identify a binding constraint rather than assuming that more capital, aid or education is always the scarce input.",
    "econ-ordoliberalism": "Ordoliberalism, associated with the Freiburg School, argues that competitive markets require an intentionally designed legal and institutional order. The state should not micromanage production, but it should protect competition, monetary stability and predictable rules while preventing private concentrations of power from undermining the market order. This differs from laissez-faire because competition is treated as an institutional achievement rather than a natural default. Ordoliberal ideas influenced postwar German social-market thinking and later European competition and monetary debates. Analytically, the approach emphasizes rules over discretionary intervention: policy should shape the framework within which private decisions occur. Critics argue that rigid rule orientation can underreact to severe macroeconomic instability.",
    "econ-physiocrats": "The Physiocrats treated agriculture as the unique source of a net economic surplus and modeled circular flows between social classes. That substantive claim did not survive industrialization, but their analytical innovations mattered. Quesnay's Tableau économique represented the economy as an interdependent flow of production and income, anticipating later social-accounting and input-output ideas. They also argued for a more unified tax system and fewer internal trade restrictions in eighteenth-century France. Their focus on a reproducible surplus influenced later classical economists. The useful lesson is historical: before modern national accounts, economists were already trying to represent how sectoral outputs and incomes reproduce the economic system over time.",
    "econ-mercantilism": "Mercantilism is a retrospective label for diverse early-modern policies emphasizing trade surpluses, bullion, navigation laws, chartered monopolies and state power. It makes more sense when analyzed in a world of frequent war, weak taxation and strategic competition: foreign-exchange earnings and shipping capacity could strengthen state finance and military power. Classical economists attacked the idea that national wealth is simply a stock of money and argued that voluntary trade can benefit both sides. Modern analysis adds a nuance: under increasing returns, strategic industries or externalities, some trade policies can alter national income, but those cases require specific market failures. The general mercantilist equation of exports with gains and imports with losses is not a valid welfare criterion.",
    "econ-schumpeter": "Schumpeter saw capitalism as an evolutionary process driven by innovation rather than a stationary equilibrium. The entrepreneur creates new combinations, and successful innovations generate temporary rents that attract imitation and reorganize industries. Banks matter because credit can finance entrepreneurs before they possess accumulated wealth. Schumpeter's later work emphasized large firms and organized research as sources of innovation, complicating the simple heroic-entrepreneur story. His creative-destruction framework also changes welfare analysis: a market that looks concentrated at one moment may still face strong dynamic competition from technologies not yet commercialized. The empirical question is whether market power finances innovation or protects incumbents from it; both patterns occur.",
    "econ-creative-destruction": "Creative destruction links productivity growth to reallocation. New technologies and business models raise the value of some skills and assets while making others obsolete, so aggregate gains can coexist with concentrated losses. The mechanism operates through entry, expansion, contraction and exit, not only through productivity improvements inside existing firms. This is why labor mobility, bankruptcy institutions, finance and retraining affect how costly technological transitions become. A dynamic competition perspective can also conflict with static antitrust measures: high current profits may be innovation rents, but persistent barriers to entry can convert temporary rents into durable monopoly. The analytical task is to distinguish contestable innovation rewards from protected incumbency.",
    "econ-georgism": "Georgism distinguishes land value from returns to produced capital. Because the supply of pure land is fixed, a tax on unimproved land value does not reduce the physical quantity of land and therefore creates less deadweight loss than taxes that discourage work or investment. The challenge is valuation: buildings and improvements must be separated from the underlying site value, and expectations about public infrastructure can be capitalized into land prices. Georgist reasoning also illuminates urban economics because access, zoning and public amenities generate location rents. A land-value tax can capture part of that socially created rent, but implementation depends on assessment quality and the broader property-tax system.",
    "econ-scarcity": "Scarcity in economics is not synonymous with physical rarity. A good is scarce when desired uses exceed what can be made available at zero opportunity cost. The analytical consequence is that every allocation has a shadow price even when no market price is charged. In constrained optimization, the Lagrange multiplier measures the marginal value of relaxing a constraint. This makes scarcity operational: an hour of engineering time, a transmission constraint or a carbon budget can all have economic scarcity values. Technological change can reduce one scarcity while creating another, so the relevant constraint is often endogenous. Economic reasoning begins by identifying the binding margin rather than assuming money is the only scarce resource.",
    "econ-opportunity-cost": "Opportunity cost is the value of the best forgone alternative, which makes it inherently marginal and choice-specific. Accounting costs can be zero while economic costs are large: using an owned building for a project sacrifices the rent that could have been earned elsewhere. In production theory, opportunity cost is encoded in the slope of a production possibility frontier; in finance it appears as the risk-adjusted return of the next-best investment. The concept also explains why sunk costs should not affect forward decisions. A common analytical error is to compare a visible cash outlay with an alternative while ignoring the resources that the chosen option displaces.",
    "econ-marginal-thinking": "Marginal analysis compares the incremental benefit and incremental cost of a small change. The optimum is usually characterized by an equality such as marginal benefit = marginal cost, but only for an interior solution and only after the correct constraint set is specified. Marginal reasoning differs from comparing averages: a firm can have positive average profit while the next unit is unprofitable, or a project can have a high historical return while new capital has a low expected return. In dynamic settings the relevant marginal cost includes discounted future effects. Many policy debates become clearer when framed around the behavioral margin actually changed by the policy rather than total activity.",
    "econ-sunk-cost": "A sunk cost is irreversible and therefore drops out of a forward-looking optimization problem. The formal logic is simple: if the cost is identical under all future choices, it cancels when alternatives are compared. Real organizations nevertheless respond to sunk costs because of reputation, career incentives, loss aversion or beliefs that abandonment reveals bad private information. That means the sunk-cost fallacy can be embedded in strategic settings where persistence itself has signaling value. The decision rule is not to ignore history completely; history matters insofar as it changes beliefs, contractual obligations or future payoffs. What should be ignored is the portion that cannot be altered and has no informational content.",
    "econ-supply": "A supply curve is a behavioral relation between price and quantity supplied, holding other determinants fixed. In competitive theory it is linked to firms' marginal costs above the shutdown condition, but the shape depends on technology, input prices, capacity and the time horizon. Short-run supply can be steep when capacity is fixed; long-run supply is typically more elastic because firms can enter, exit or invest. With market power there is generally no unique supply curve independent of demand, because the firm's optimal quantity depends on the demand curve it faces. Treating supply as a universal physical schedule therefore hides the institutional assumptions that generate it.",
    "econ-demand": "Demand is derived from willingness to pay under income and budget constraints. A price change generates both substitution and income effects, which can point in opposite directions. Hicksian demand holds utility constant and isolates substitution, while Marshallian demand reflects the actual budget effect; the Slutsky equation links the two. Market demand aggregates heterogeneous consumers, so elasticities can differ substantially across income groups and time horizons. Observed quantity-price correlations do not identify a demand curve unless supply shifts provide variation or another identification strategy is available. This is a fundamental econometric point: equilibrium data combine supply and demand simultaneously.",
    "econ-equilibrium": "Market equilibrium is a consistency condition, not a claim that the economy is morally desirable or literally motionless. In a simple competitive market, the equilibrium price equates desired purchases and sales. In game theory, equilibrium means strategies are mutually consistent; in general equilibrium, all markets clear simultaneously subject to endowments and technology. Stability is a separate question: an equilibrium can exist but not be reached by plausible adjustment dynamics. Efficiency is also separate and requires additional assumptions. The value of equilibrium analysis is disciplined counterfactual reasoning: if a parameter changes, which prices and quantities must adjust so that individual plans again become mutually compatible?",
    "econ-elasticity": "Elasticity is a unit-free measure of responsiveness, typically percentage change in quantity divided by percentage change in a determinant. Because it is local, elasticity can vary along a nonlinear demand or supply curve. It is central to incidence: the side of a market that is less elastic bears more of a tax because it has fewer margins of adjustment. Elasticity also changes with time; gasoline demand, for example, may be inelastic over weeks but more elastic over years when vehicle choice and location can adjust. Estimating elasticity requires exogenous variation, since observed prices are equilibrium outcomes. Policy analysis therefore depends as much on credible identification as on the textbook formula.",
    "econ-consumer-surplus": "Consumer surplus measures the area between willingness to pay and the market price. It is a money-metric approximation to welfare and is exact only under restrictive conditions; for large price changes with income effects, compensating or equivalent variation is theoretically cleaner. Surplus measures also omit distributional weights unless the analyst adds them explicitly: one euro of surplus to a high-income consumer and one euro to a low-income consumer count equally in standard cost-benefit analysis. Despite these limits, consumer surplus is powerful for evaluating taxes, regulation and market design because it converts heterogeneous willingness to pay into a common metric. The key is not to confuse willingness to pay with a complete measure of social value.",
    "econ-producer-surplus": "Producer surplus is revenue minus variable opportunity costs and corresponds to the area above the supply curve and below price in a competitive setting. It differs from accounting profit because fixed costs and the opportunity cost of owned capital may be treated differently. In the short run, a firm can earn positive producer surplus yet negative economic profit if fixed costs are large. In the long run, entry can erode rents in competitive industries. Producer-surplus changes are useful for incidence analysis, but they do not reveal who ultimately receives the gain: shareholders, workers, landowners and suppliers can share it depending on contractual and market structure.",
    "econ-price-ceiling": "A binding price ceiling sets the legal price below the market-clearing level. The textbook shortage is only the first-order effect. Non-price rationing then becomes important: queues, search, quality reduction, side payments or landlord screening can allocate the scarce quantity. Long-run supply responses can make the shortage larger than the initial static diagram suggests. Welfare effects also depend on who receives the controlled good; if rationing shifts units toward consumers with lower willingness to pay, allocative inefficiency can exceed the lost trades alone. In markets such as housing, empirical effects vary with coverage, exemptions and whether new construction is controlled, so policy design matters more than the label.",
    "econ-price-floor": "A binding price floor prevents the price from falling to the market-clearing level. If the government does not purchase the excess supply, quantity traded is determined by the short side of the market, and sellers may compete on non-price dimensions. In labor markets a minimum wage is a price floor, but the competitive model is not the only relevant benchmark: with monopsony power, a moderate wage floor can increase both wages and employment. The sign of the employment effect therefore depends on market structure, compliance and the level of the floor. The general lesson is that price controls must be analyzed with the appropriate model of how the market actually clears.",
    "econ-tax-incidence": "Economic incidence is determined by behavioral responses, not by who legally remits a tax. In a competitive market, the less elastic side bears more of the burden because it cannot easily change quantity. With perfectly inelastic supply, suppliers bear the tax regardless of statutory assignment; with perfectly elastic supply, buyers bear it. In labor and capital markets, incidence can shift through wages, prices, rents and returns over long horizons. Market power, international mobility and general-equilibrium effects complicate the simple diagram. A good incidence analysis specifies the time horizon and the margin of adjustment instead of treating the legal taxpayer as the economic payer.",
    "econ-deadweight-loss": "Deadweight loss is the value of mutually beneficial transactions or activities that disappear because a wedge separates private marginal benefit from private marginal cost. For a small tax in a competitive market, the loss rises approximately with the square of the tax rate because both the wedge and the behavioral response grow. That does not make taxes socially undesirable by definition: tax revenue finances public purposes and can correct externalities. The welfare question is the marginal excess burden relative to the value of revenue raised. With pre-existing distortions, the interaction between taxes matters, so adding a tax can have larger or smaller effects than an isolated partial-equilibrium triangle suggests.",
    "econ-externality": "An externality exists when an action changes another agent's welfare or production possibilities through a channel not priced in the decision. The Pigouvian benchmark sets a tax equal to marginal external damage, aligning private and social marginal costs. Coasean bargaining can internalize the effect when property rights are clear and transaction costs low, but those conditions often fail when many parties are affected. Externalities can be positive, negative, technological or informational. The difficult empirical problem is measuring the marginal external effect rather than the average harm. Policy design must also account for monitoring, evasion, distribution and uncertainty about damages.",
    "econ-public-good": "A pure public good is non-rival and non-excludable. Efficiency requires summing individuals' marginal willingness to pay vertically because everyone consumes the same quantity. Voluntary provision tends to underprovide when individuals can free ride on others' contributions, but repeated interaction, social norms and selective incentives can partially overcome the problem. Public provision does not automatically solve valuation: governments still need information about preferences and face budget constraints. Many real goods are impure public goods with congestion or partial excludability, which changes the optimal institution. The analytical task is to identify which dimension creates the market failure rather than labeling any publicly supplied service a public good.",
    "econ-common-resource": "Common-pool resources are rival but difficult to exclude users from. That combination creates an extraction externality: each user captures the private benefit of harvesting while sharing part of the depletion cost with others. Open access can therefore drive effort beyond the surplus-maximizing level. Privatization is one solution, but it is not the only one. Community governance, quotas, tradable permits and territorial rights can work when monitoring and enforcement are feasible. Elinor Ostrom's work showed that users can sometimes build durable rules without either pure privatization or centralized control. The correct institution depends on mobility of the resource, user heterogeneity and enforcement costs.",
    "econ-asymmetric-info": "Asymmetric information changes who enters a market and how contracts are designed. When one side knows a relevant characteristic the other cannot observe, prices can affect the composition of participants rather than only quantity. In Akerlof's lemons model, a lower market price drives high-quality sellers out, worsening average quality and potentially causing market unraveling. The general response is to create information through warranties, certification, reputation, collateral, deductibles or screening menus. These devices are not free; they can distort behavior in order to separate types. Information economics therefore explains why real contracts often look deliberately complicated rather than simply specifying a price.",
    "econ-adverse-selection": "Adverse selection is hidden information before a contract or transaction. In insurance, high-risk individuals value coverage more, so raising premiums can disproportionately drive low-risk customers out and further worsen the risk pool. In credit, borrowers willing to pay very high rates may also be riskier, so a bank may ration credit instead of simply raising the interest rate. Screening can mitigate selection by offering contracts that induce different types to self-select, such as high-deductible versus low-deductible insurance. The efficiency cost comes from distorted participation and contract terms, not merely from inaccurate average pricing.",
    "econ-moral-hazard": "Moral hazard arises when a contract changes behavior after the contract is signed because actions are imperfectly observable. Insurance can reduce incentives to prevent losses; limited liability can encourage risk taking; employment contracts face effort-monitoring problems. Optimal contracts therefore trade off risk sharing against incentives. Deductibles, co-payments, collateral, bonuses and monitoring are all mechanisms for retaining some marginal consequence of behavior. Moral hazard does not mean fraud or bad character; it is an incentive effect created by the structure of payoffs. Empirical identification requires distinguishing behavior changes from adverse selection, since high claims can reflect either riskier types or riskier actions.",
    "econ-principal-agent": "A principal-agent problem arises when one party delegates a task to another whose objectives differ and whose actions or information cannot be perfectly observed. Contract theory asks how compensation, monitoring and decision rights can align incentives while recognizing risk and information constraints. High-powered incentives can improve measured effort but distort unmeasured dimensions, producing multitasking problems. Corporate governance, executive pay, franchise contracts and public procurement all fit this framework. The deeper point is that delegation creates an endogenous organizational problem: the optimal contract is shaped by what can be measured, verified and enforced, not simply by what the principal wishes the agent to do.",
    "econ-transaction-cost": "Transaction costs include search, bargaining, measurement, monitoring, enforcement and adaptation. Coase's insight is that these costs help determine whether an activity is coordinated through markets, long-term contracts or managerial authority inside a firm. Asset specificity is especially important: when an investment has much less value outside one relationship, the investor becomes vulnerable to hold-up after the investment is sunk. Governance arrangements can protect quasi-rents but may create bureaucracy and weaker incentives. Transaction-cost economics is therefore comparative: no institution is costless, and the relevant question is which governance structure best fits the attributes of the transaction.",
    "econ-property-rights": "Property rights specify who may use an asset, exclude others, transfer it and receive residual returns. Clear rights can strengthen investment incentives because decision-makers expect to capture more of the payoff from maintenance or improvement. But rights are bundles rather than all-or-nothing ownership, and enforcement is costly. In incomplete-contract theory, ownership matters because it gives residual control over uses not specified in the contract. The efficiency effect can be ambiguous when multiple parties make relationship-specific investments: assigning control to one party strengthens its incentives while weakening another's. Property-rights analysis therefore focuses on control, enforcement and external effects rather than treating ownership as a binary ideological category.",
    "econ-network-effects": "A direct network effect means a user's value rises with the number of other users on the same network; indirect effects arise when a larger installed base attracts complementary products. These mechanisms can create tipping, path dependence and high switching costs. They also complicate competition policy because concentration may reflect genuine coordination benefits while simultaneously raising entry barriers. Multi-homing can weaken lock-in if users can participate in several networks at low cost. Platform markets add cross-side effects: more buyers attract sellers and vice versa, so optimal pricing may subsidize one side. Static market-share measures can therefore miss the competitive role of ecosystem structure and interoperability.",
    "econ-economies-scale": "Economies of scale mean average cost falls as output rises. They can come from indivisible fixed costs, specialized machinery, learning, purchasing power or network infrastructure. Internal scale economies occur within the firm and can produce natural-concentration forces; external scale economies arise at the industry or regional level through shared suppliers, labor pools or knowledge spillovers. Scale differs from scope, which concerns cost savings from producing multiple products jointly. A key empirical question is the minimum efficient scale relative to total market demand. If efficient scale is large, fragmented competition may be costly, but concentrated production raises market-power and regulatory issues.",
    "econ-diminishing-returns": "Diminishing marginal returns occur when one input increases while at least one other input is fixed, causing the extra output from the variable input eventually to fall. This is a short-run technological statement, not the same as decreasing returns to scale, where all inputs rise proportionally. The distinction matters because a factory can exhibit diminishing marginal product of labor at a fixed capital stock while still having constant returns to scale when capital expands too. Marginal product also underpins factor-demand conditions: a competitive firm hires labor until the value of the marginal product equals the wage. In practice, organizational congestion and capacity constraints often generate diminishing returns before physical limits do.",
    "econ-perfect-competition": "Perfect competition is a benchmark built from price-taking, homogeneous products, free entry and sufficiently complete information. Individual firms face a horizontal demand curve and choose output where price equals marginal cost, while entry pushes long-run economic profit toward zero. The welfare theorems add further assumptions such as complete markets and no externalities. The model is valuable because deviations become diagnostically meaningful: markups indicate market power, wedges indicate taxes or frictions, and missing markets signal incomplete contracting. Real industries need not resemble the benchmark literally for it to be useful, but policy conclusions should not be imported from the benchmark without checking the relevant departures.",
    "econ-monopoly": "A monopolist chooses quantity or price recognizing the downward-sloping demand curve. The optimal markup is linked to demand elasticity: the less elastic demand is, the larger the profit-maximizing wedge between price and marginal cost. Monopoly can create deadweight loss by restricting output, but dynamic effects complicate welfare. Patent protection, for example, trades off current markup distortions against incentives to innovate. Natural monopoly arises when scale economies make one producer cost-efficient over the relevant demand range, shifting the policy question toward regulation, franchise competition or access rules. Measuring market power requires defining the relevant market and distinguishing temporary innovation rents from durable barriers to entry.",
    "econ-oligopoly": "Oligopoly requires strategic reasoning because each firm's best action depends on rivals' choices. Cournot models quantity competition, Bertrand models price competition, and differentiated-product models combine prices with imperfect substitution. The result can vary sharply with timing, capacity and product differentiation. Repeated interaction can sustain tacit coordination even without explicit collusion when firms can punish deviations. Entry threats and buyer power also matter. Modern empirical industrial organization often estimates demand and costs to simulate mergers or policy changes rather than relying on concentration alone. A concentrated market can be highly competitive, while a less concentrated market can sustain large markups if switching costs or differentiation are strong.",
    "econ-game-theory": "Game theory formalizes strategic interdependence: each player's payoff depends on others' actions. A game requires players, strategies, information, timing and payoffs. Different solution concepts fit different environments. Dominant strategies do not depend on opponents' actions; Nash equilibrium requires mutual best responses; subgame-perfect equilibrium rules out non-credible threats in dynamic games. Repeated games can support cooperation that a one-shot game cannot. Information structure is crucial: incomplete-information games model uncertainty about types, while signaling games allow informed players to take costly actions. The framework is not a prediction machine unless the game has been specified correctly; changing timing or information can change the equilibrium completely.",
    "econ-nash-equilibrium": "A Nash equilibrium is a strategy profile in which no player can gain by deviating unilaterally given the strategies of others. It is a consistency concept, not necessarily an efficient or unique outcome. Some games have multiple Nash equilibria, creating an equilibrium-selection problem; others require mixed strategies. In dynamic games, a Nash equilibrium can rely on threats that would not be optimal if the threatened node were actually reached, motivating refinements such as subgame perfection. In markets, Nash reasoning underlies models of oligopoly, auctions and bargaining. The practical analytical question is whether the assumed strategies are mutually stable under unilateral deviation, not whether the outcome appears fair or socially desirable.",
    "econ-prisoners-dilemma": "The prisoner's dilemma shows how individually dominant actions can produce a Pareto-inferior collective outcome. The structure is defined by payoffs, not by the story: each player prefers to defect regardless of the other's action, yet both would be better off under mutual cooperation. Repetition changes the game because current behavior can affect future responses, allowing cooperation when players value the future sufficiently and can observe deviations. Institutions can also alter payoffs through enforcement, reputation or selective incentives. The model is useful for pollution, arms races and common-resource problems, but only when the payoff ordering truly fits; many coordination problems are better represented by different games.",
    "econ-comparative-advantage": "Comparative advantage is determined by relative opportunity costs. If country A is more productive in both goods, trade can still create gains if A's productivity advantage is relatively larger in one good. In the Ricardian model, specialization reallocates labor toward the sector with lower relative unit labor cost. Modern models add multiple factors, transport costs, heterogeneous firms and nontraded goods. Aggregate gains can arise from specialization, variety, scale and productivity selection, but distributional effects are separate. Trade changes relative factor demand and can create persistent regional losses even when national real income rises. The theorem establishes a potential efficiency gain, not a claim that adjustment is costless or compensation automatically occurs.",
    "econ-gdp": "GDP measures the market value of final production within a territory over a period. The expenditure, income and production approaches are accounting views of the same activity. GDP excludes most nonmarket household production and does not deduct environmental depletion or inequality, so it is not a direct welfare index. It also treats defensive spending and reconstruction as production. Yet GDP remains useful because it provides a coherent aggregate of market activity and a denominator for debt, taxes and productivity. Analysts must distinguish nominal GDP from real GDP and level from growth. Cross-country comparisons additionally require purchasing-power adjustments when the goal is living standards rather than market-exchange-rate output.",
    "econ-real-gdp": "Real GDP attempts to isolate changes in quantities from changes in prices. Modern statistical agencies typically use chain-weighted volume indexes because a fixed base-year basket becomes distorted as relative prices change. Real GDP is therefore an index rather than a literal sum of physical quantities. Quality adjustment is difficult for rapidly improving goods and digital services, and revisions can be substantial. Real GDP growth is central for cycle analysis, but per-capita and per-hour measures answer different questions. A country can have strong aggregate real GDP growth due to population growth while productivity or living standards rise much more slowly.",
    "econ-gdp-per-capita": "GDP per capita divides output by population and is often used as a proxy for average material living standards. It improves on total GDP for cross-country comparison but still says nothing about distribution: median household resources can diverge from the mean when inequality changes. Purchasing-power-parity conversion is usually preferable for comparing consumption possibilities across countries because nontraded prices differ systematically. Per-capita GDP also excludes leisure, household production, environmental quality and many public goods. For growth analysis, the key decomposition is output per person into labor utilization and output per hour; long-run differences are ultimately tied heavily to productivity.",
    "econ-inflation": "Inflation is a sustained increase in the general price level, not a one-time rise in a single price. Different indexes answer different questions: CPI tracks a consumer basket, the GDP deflator covers domestically produced final goods, and core measures strip volatile components to reveal persistence. Inflation redistributes between debtors and creditors when unexpected, distorts relative-price signals when adjustment is uneven, and interacts with nominal tax rules. The macroeconomic challenge is persistence: supply shocks can raise prices once, but sustained inflation usually requires wage, pricing and expectation dynamics that keep nominal growth elevated. Central banks therefore monitor expectations and labor costs rather than reacting mechanically to every relative-price shock.",
    "econ-deflation": "Deflation can be damaging when it is persistent and unexpected because nominal debts do not automatically shrink with the price level. Falling prices raise the real burden of debt, which can force deleveraging and depress spending, a mechanism associated with Fisher's debt-deflation theory. Deflation also raises the real interest rate for a given nominal policy rate, which is especially problematic near the effective lower bound. Not every price decline is contractionary: productivity-driven declines in specific goods can coexist with strong growth. The risk comes from broad deflation interacting with weak demand, leveraged balance sheets and expectations of further price declines.",
    "econ-stagflation": "Stagflation combines weak real activity with high inflation, challenging models in which inflation is driven only by excess demand. Supply shocks, adverse productivity movements and unanchored expectations can shift the inflation-output relationship. The 1970s episodes reflected oil shocks, wage-price dynamics, policy mistakes and changing expectations rather than a single mechanism. For policymakers, stagflation creates a tradeoff: tightening demand reduces inflation pressure but can deepen unemployment, while supporting activity can prolong inflation. Modern central banks therefore emphasize credibility and distinguish temporary relative-price shocks from persistent second-round effects. The episode also helped motivate expectations-augmented Phillips curves and later New Classical and New Keynesian frameworks.",
    "econ-unemployment": "Unemployment is a stock-flow phenomenon. The unemployment rate can change because job finding changes, separations change, or workers enter and leave the labor force. Search-and-matching models therefore analyze vacancies, matching efficiency and bargaining rather than assuming a single market-clearing wage. Cyclical unemployment rises when aggregate demand weakens, while structural unemployment reflects persistent mismatches or institutions. Measurement also matters: discouraged workers who stop searching are not counted as unemployed. A complete labor-market diagnosis uses participation, employment-population ratios, hours, vacancies and wage growth alongside the headline unemployment rate.",
    "econ-natural-rate": "The natural rate is the unemployment rate consistent with stable inflation after temporary demand effects dissipate. It is not observable and is not fixed. Demographics, matching efficiency, bargaining institutions, benefit systems and labor-market hysteresis can shift it. In expectations-augmented Phillips curves, unemployment below the natural rate creates upward inflation pressure if expectations are given. Modern central banks often use a related concept, the non-accelerating inflation rate of unemployment, but estimates are highly uncertain and revised. Treating a point estimate as a hard structural constant can lead to policy errors, especially after major shocks that change participation or matching.",
    "econ-business-cycle": "Business cycles are co-movements in output, employment, income, investment and credit around longer-run trends. Investment and durable consumption are typically more volatile than nondurable consumption because they can be postponed. Competing models emphasize different shocks and propagation mechanisms: technology, monetary policy, demand, credit conditions, uncertainty or inventory dynamics. The challenge is distinguishing the initial shock from the mechanisms that amplify and persist it. Financial leverage can turn a small asset-price decline into a large contraction, while nominal rigidities can make demand shocks affect real output. No single model explains every cycle equally well, so diagnosis depends on the observed configuration of inflation, labor markets, credit and sectoral stress.",
    "econ-recession": "A recession is a broad contraction in economic activity, not simply two negative quarters in every statistical system. The mechanism matters more than the label. A demand-driven recession with falling inflation calls for different analysis from a supply-driven contraction with high inflation, and a balance-sheet recession behaves differently from an ordinary inventory cycle. Automatic stabilizers cushion disposable income without new legislation, while monetary easing works through rates, asset prices, credit and expectations. Recovery can be slow when bankruptcies destroy organizational capital or long-term unemployment erodes matches. This persistence is one reason economists study hysteresis rather than assuming output instantly returns to its pre-shock path.",
    "econ-aggregate-demand": "Aggregate demand is the relationship between the price level or inflation environment and total desired expenditure, but the mechanism differs across models. In an IS-LM interpretation, lower interest rates stimulate interest-sensitive spending. In modern intertemporal models, the key variable is the real interest rate relative to households' and firms' desired intertemporal substitution. Fiscal policy, foreign demand, credit conditions and wealth also shift demand. Because one person's spending is another's income, expenditure changes can be amplified when unused capacity is available. The aggregate-demand curve should therefore not be interpreted as a simple horizontal sum of individual demand curves for one good.",
    "econ-aggregate-supply": "Aggregate supply links inflation or the price level to real activity. In the short run, nominal rigidities, capacity utilization and labor-market tightness allow output to vary with demand. In the long run, sustainable output is constrained by labor, capital, productivity and institutions. Supply shocks such as energy disruptions can reduce output while raising inflation, unlike pure demand shocks. Modern Phillips-curve models often replace a literal aggregate-supply curve with a relationship between inflation, expected inflation and marginal cost or the output gap. The practical issue is how quickly supply capacity adjusts and whether a shock changes potential output or merely temporarily raises costs.",
    "econ-multiplier": "The fiscal multiplier is the change in output associated with a change in government spending or taxes. It is not a universal constant. It depends on monetary-policy response, exchange-rate regime, slack, household liquidity constraints, import leakage and the persistence and composition of the intervention. When the central bank offsets fiscal expansion with higher rates, the multiplier can be small; near an effective lower bound with idle resources, it can be larger. Transfer multipliers depend on recipients' marginal propensities to consume. Empirical estimates are difficult because government spending often responds to the economy, so identification commonly relies on military shocks, institutional rules or forecast errors.",
    "econ-output-gap": "The output gap is actual output minus estimated potential output, usually expressed as a percentage of potential. A negative gap signals slack; a positive gap suggests activity above a sustainable noninflationary pace. The difficulty is that potential output is unobserved. Statistical filters can mistake a persistent demand slump for a decline in potential, while production-function methods require estimates of sustainable labor and productivity. Revisions are often large. Policymakers therefore combine gap estimates with inflation, wage growth, utilization and labor-market indicators. The concept is useful as a framework for resource pressure, but false precision is dangerous.",
    "econ-phillips-curve": "The Phillips curve describes a relationship between inflation dynamics and economic slack, but its form has changed over time. Modern versions typically include expected inflation and a measure of marginal cost or the output gap. If expectations become unanchored, the short-run tradeoff shifts. The curve can also appear flat when central banks systematically offset demand shocks: successful stabilization reduces observed correlation between inflation and unemployment. Supply shocks add further noise. The policy implication is not that policymakers can choose any permanent inflation-unemployment combination; long-run unemployment is determined largely by real labor-market factors, while persistent inflation depends on nominal dynamics and expectations.",
    "econ-expectations": "Inflation expectations affect wage bargaining, price setting, long-term interest rates and real borrowing costs. Economists distinguish household surveys, professional forecasts and market-implied measures because each captures different information and risk premia. Expectations can be backward-looking, model-consistent or formed with limited information. Central-bank credibility matters because well-anchored long-run expectations reduce the risk that temporary supply shocks feed into persistent wage-price dynamics. But observed expectations are not pure forecasts: market breakevens embed liquidity and inflation risk premia. Policy analysis should therefore compare multiple measures and ask which agents' expectations matter for the transmission mechanism under study.",
    "econ-productivity": "Productivity measures output relative to inputs. Labor productivity can rise because workers become more skilled, firms use more capital per worker, or total factor productivity improves. Long-run living standards depend heavily on productivity because employment and hours per person have natural limits. Productivity growth can come from technology, management, reallocation toward more efficient firms and scale. Measurement is difficult in services and digital products where quality changes rapidly. Temporary cyclical movements also distort productivity because firms do not adjust labor instantly. For growth analysis, the central question is whether a productivity improvement is a one-time level shift or a persistent increase in the growth rate.",
    "econ-tfp": "Total factor productivity is the residual portion of output growth not accounted for by measured growth in capital and labor, after weighting inputs by their income shares in a standard growth-accounting framework. Calling it technology is convenient but too strong. TFP also captures measurement error, utilization changes, management, reallocation and model misspecification. In levels, cross-country TFP gaps can reflect institutions and misallocation as well as engineering knowledge. Because it is a residual, TFP should be interpreted as an index of efficiency and unmeasured factors rather than a directly observed technological variable. Its importance comes from the arithmetic fact that capital deepening alone faces diminishing returns.",
    "econ-potential-output": "Potential output is the level of production consistent with sustainable use of labor and capital without continuously accelerating inflation. It is not the maximum physically possible output. Estimates depend on trend productivity, labor-force participation, capital stock and the equilibrium unemployment rate. Severe recessions can reduce potential through lost investment, bankruptcies or hysteresis, while strong demand can sometimes reveal that previous estimates were too pessimistic by drawing workers back into employment. Because potential is inferred rather than observed, real-time policy must operate under uncertainty. This makes robustness across multiple indicators more important than a single estimated output-gap number.",
    "econ-money-functions": "Money performs several functions: medium of exchange, unit of account and store of value. These functions can separate. Bank deposits can be excellent transaction media while long-duration government bonds may be better stores of value in some environments. The unit-of-account role is especially important because contracts and prices are denominated in nominal units, creating monetary non-neutrality when prices adjust slowly. Modern monetary systems are layered: central-bank liabilities settle payments between banks, while commercial-bank deposits dominate money held by households and firms. The boundary of money is therefore partly functional, which is why different monetary aggregates can behave differently as financial innovation changes liquidity.",
    "econ-fractional-banking": "A bank balance sheet has loans and securities on the asset side and deposits, wholesale funding and equity on the liability side. Calling this fractional-reserve banking can obscure the modern constraint set. Banks generally make profitable loans first and obtain the reserves needed for settlement afterward, subject to capital, liquidity, risk management and central-bank policy. Equity absorbs losses; reserves are settlement assets, not a simple lending fund. A bank can be solvent but illiquid if assets exceed liabilities yet cash cannot be raised quickly enough. Conversely, abundant reserves do not make an insolvent bank healthy. Balance-sheet analysis clarifies why capital regulation and liquidity regulation solve different problems.",
    "econ-money-creation": "Commercial-bank lending creates money by simultaneously booking a loan asset and a deposit liability. The banking system does not need to collect an equal amount of prior deposits before every new loan, although individual banks must manage funding and settlement outflows. Central banks accommodate reserve demand at the policy rate needed to implement monetary policy. This makes the textbook money multiplier an unreliable causal description in systems with interest-rate targeting. The real constraints on money creation are credit demand, borrower quality, bank capital, liquidity, regulation and expected profitability. Loan repayment destroys deposits, reversing the balance-sheet expansion. The distinction between central-bank money and bank deposits is therefore fundamental.",
    "econ-central-bank": "A central bank anchors the monetary system by issuing the settlement asset used between banks and setting conditions in short-term money markets. Its conventional tool is the policy rate, but it also operates payment systems, supplies emergency liquidity and often supervises banks. Independence is designed to reduce time-inconsistency and political pressure for short-run stimulus, yet mandates and accountability remain political choices. Modern central banks implement policy through administered rates on reserves or corridor systems rather than by fixing a quantity of money. Their influence is powerful but indirect: transmission depends on expectations, bank lending, asset prices, exchange rates and the sensitivity of private spending to financing conditions.",
    "econ-policy-rate": "The policy rate is an overnight or short-term interest rate controlled closely by the central bank. Its macroeconomic effect comes from the expected path of future short rates, not only today's setting. Bond yields embed expected future policy plus term premia, while bank loan rates add credit and funding spreads. A tighter policy path raises real borrowing costs if inflation expectations do not rise equally, reducing interest-sensitive demand and typically cooling labor and product markets with a lag. The neutral real rate is unobservable, so the same nominal rate can be restrictive in one environment and expansionary in another. Monetary stance should therefore be judged relative to inflation, expectations and equilibrium rates.",
    "econ-quantitative-easing": "Quantitative easing changes the composition and duration of assets held by the private sector when a central bank buys long-term securities and creates reserves. The main channels are term-premium compression, portfolio rebalancing, signaling about future policy and market-function support. QE is not equivalent to handing households spendable cash; reserves are bank assets, and the transmission to spending depends on yields, credit and expectations. Its effects are generally larger when markets are impaired or policy rates are near their lower bound. Central-bank asset purchases can also create fiscal-like duration risk on the consolidated public balance sheet, because short-duration reserve liabilities replace longer-duration government debt.",
    "econ-quantitative-tightening": "Quantitative tightening reduces a central bank's securities holdings, usually by allowing assets to mature without reinvestment or by selling them. As reserves decline, the financial system moves from abundant liquidity toward scarcer reserve conditions. QT can raise term premia or tighten market liquidity, but its effects are usually less mechanically predictable than a policy-rate increase because reserve demand and dealer balance sheets matter. The central bank must avoid shrinking its balance sheet below the level needed for smooth payment settlement and rate control. QT is therefore both a monetary-policy operation and a market-structure problem involving the distribution of liquidity across institutions.",
    "econ-yield-curve": "The yield curve plots interest rates by maturity. Under the expectations hypothesis, a long yield reflects expected future short rates, but observed yields also include term premia for duration risk. A curve can invert because markets expect future policy easing, because term premia fall, or both. This is why an inversion is informative about macro expectations without being a deterministic recession mechanism. Different maturities respond to different combinations of policy expectations, inflation risk and safe-asset demand. For valuation, the curve provides discount factors; for banking, its shape affects maturity-transformation profitability but must be considered alongside credit spreads and hedging.",
    "econ-real-interest": "The ex ante real interest rate is approximately the nominal rate minus expected inflation, while the ex post real rate subtracts realized inflation. Decisions depend on the ex ante rate because borrowers and lenders act before future inflation is known. Real rates allocate consumption across time and influence investment by changing discount rates. The equilibrium or natural real rate is the rate consistent with output at potential and stable inflation absent temporary shocks, but it is unobservable. Demographics, productivity, inequality, safe-asset demand and global saving can shift it. Monetary policy is restrictive or expansionary relative to this moving benchmark rather than relative to zero.",
    "econ-compound-interest": "Compound interest means returns are earned on prior returns, generating exponential rather than linear accumulation. If a continuously compounded rate is r, value grows approximately as e^(rt); under discrete compounding it grows as (1+r)^t. The same mathematics magnifies debt burdens, not only investment wealth. Comparing rates therefore requires consistent compounding conventions and inflation adjustment. Small differences in annual returns become large over long horizons, but so do fees, taxes and volatility drag. Expected compound growth is also not equal to the arithmetic average return when returns are volatile; the geometric mean is lower because losses require proportionally larger gains to recover.",
    "econ-present-value": "Present value discounts future cash flows because resources available at different dates are not economically equivalent. For a cash flow C_t and discount rate r_t, value is the sum of C_t divided by appropriate discount factors. The difficult part is choosing the rate. Risk-free cash flows should be discounted differently from risky cash flows, and the maturity structure matters. In asset pricing, discount rates reflect time value plus compensation for systematic risk, not simply uncertainty. Present-value identities also show why asset prices can fall sharply when required returns rise even if expected future cash flows are unchanged. Long-duration assets are especially sensitive because more value arrives far in the future.",
    "econ-risk-return": "Higher expected return is compensation only for risks that cannot be cheaply diversified away. In standard asset pricing, idiosyncratic firm-specific risk should not command a persistent premium because diversified investors can remove it. Systematic risk matters because it covaries with states in which investors' marginal utility of wealth is high. This logic underlies beta models and more general stochastic discount-factor approaches. Historical average returns are noisy estimates of expected returns, especially for rare disasters and long-duration assets. A high realized return can reflect luck rather than compensation for risk. Risk therefore has to be defined in relation to the investor's portfolio and economic state, not by standalone volatility alone.",
    "econ-diversification": "Diversification reduces portfolio variance when asset returns are imperfectly correlated. The gain depends more on covariance than on the number of holdings: fifty firms exposed to the same factor can still form a concentrated portfolio. In mean-variance analysis, efficient portfolios maximize expected return for a given variance, but real investors also face tail risk, liquidity risk and parameter uncertainty. Correlations often rise during crises, reducing diversification exactly when it is most desired. International diversification can add currency risk, while hedging that currency exposure changes the return distribution. The core principle is to diversify across independent sources of risk rather than merely across labels.",
    "econ-bond-price-yield": "A conventional bond's price is the discounted value of coupons and principal, so price moves inversely with yield. Duration approximates the percentage price sensitivity to a small yield change, while convexity captures curvature for larger moves. A long-maturity low-coupon bond usually has greater duration than a short-maturity high-coupon bond. Yield changes can reflect risk-free rates, expected inflation, term premia or credit spreads, each with different economic meaning. Holding to maturity eliminates mark-to-market uncertainty only if the issuer does not default and the investor does not need to sell; reinvestment risk and opportunity cost remain.",
    "econ-bank-run": "A bank run can occur because deposits are short-term promises while bank assets are longer-term and illiquid. Even a solvent bank may fail if too many depositors demand cash simultaneously and assets must be sold at fire-sale prices. Diamond-Dybvig models show how multiple equilibria can make runs partly self-fulfilling, while modern runs also occur through wholesale funding and digital transfers. Liquidity insurance from a central bank can stop panic when assets are fundamentally sound, but indiscriminate support creates moral hazard. The policy problem is to distinguish liquidity stress from insolvency quickly enough to prevent contagion without socializing persistent losses.",
    "econ-deposit-insurance": "Deposit insurance reduces incentives for retail depositors to run by guaranteeing eligible balances. That stabilizes the liability side of banks but weakens depositor discipline, creating moral hazard if banks can take risk while funding remains insured. The regulatory complement is therefore capital requirements, supervision and risk-based insurance premiums. Coverage limits matter: very broad guarantees can stabilize crises but expand contingent public liabilities. Insurance also changes competitive dynamics because small banks can fund at rates less sensitive to perceived risk. The design problem is a tradeoff between preventing destructive runs and preserving incentives for prudent balance-sheet management.",
    "econ-lender-last-resort": "A lender of last resort supplies temporary liquidity to solvent institutions that cannot obtain funding privately during panic. The classic Bagehot prescription is often summarized as lending freely, against good collateral, at a penalty rate, though modern crisis practice is more complex. Emergency lending prevents forced asset sales from turning a liquidity shock into system-wide insolvency. The difficulty is information: solvency is hard to assess during a crisis, and support can protect creditors who took excessive risk. Effective facilities therefore combine collateral rules, supervisory information, capital backstops and resolution regimes. Liquidity support cannot restore a bank whose assets are fundamentally worth less than its liabilities.",
    "econ-financial-leverage": "Leverage uses debt to finance assets, magnifying the return on equity because a smaller equity base absorbs the same asset gain or loss. If assets rise 5 percent, a highly leveraged investor may earn a much larger percentage return on equity; if assets fall, equity is erased quickly. Leverage also creates nonlinear dynamics because lenders impose margin calls, haircuts or covenants when collateral values fall. Forced deleveraging then pushes asset prices lower, tightening constraints further. This feedback is central to financial crises. Evaluating leverage therefore requires examining asset volatility, funding maturity, collateral terms and liquidity, not only the debt-to-equity ratio.",
    "econ-liquidity": "Liquidity has several meanings that should be separated. Market liquidity concerns the ability to trade quickly with little price impact; funding liquidity concerns the ability to obtain cash or financing; balance-sheet liquidity concerns holdings of cash-like assets. These dimensions interact. When funding dries up, leveraged institutions sell assets, reducing market liquidity and collateral values, which can further reduce funding. A liquid asset in normal times can become illiquid during stress if all holders attempt to sell simultaneously. This is why liquidity regulation and central-bank facilities focus on stress scenarios rather than average turnover alone.",
    "econ-credit-cycle": "Credit cycles arise when lending conditions, asset prices and borrower balance sheets reinforce one another. During expansions, rising collateral values reduce measured leverage and defaults, encouraging further lending. Credit can then finance asset purchases that push collateral values higher. When prices reverse, leverage rises mechanically, lenders tighten standards and borrowers deleverage, amplifying the downturn. This financial-accelerator logic explains why identical real shocks can have different effects depending on starting balance sheets. Credit growth is therefore informative about vulnerability, but not every credit boom ends in crisis. Composition, underwriting standards, funding structure and the assets being financed matter.",
    "econ-absolute-advantage": "Absolute advantage asks who can produce a good with fewer resources. It is relevant for productivity levels but does not determine the pattern of trade by itself. If one country has an absolute advantage in every product, it still cannot specialize in everything because resources are limited. Relative opportunity costs determine which activities it gives up least by specializing. Absolute advantage matters for wage levels and total productive capacity, while comparative advantage governs allocation across sectors under the simple Ricardian model. Confusing the two leads to the mistaken belief that a less productive country has nothing to export.",
    "econ-trade-gains": "Gains from trade come from several channels: specialization according to comparative advantage, access to greater product variety, exploitation of scale economies, stronger competition and reallocation toward more productive firms. These are aggregate gains; distribution is a separate problem. Import competition can lower consumer prices while reducing earnings in exposed regions, and adjustment may be slow because workers and capital are not frictionlessly mobile. Trade policy should therefore distinguish efficiency from distribution. Compensation is theoretically possible but institutionally difficult. Modern empirical trade research often measures welfare using changes in prices and expenditure shares rather than assuming the gains are evenly spread.",
    "econ-tariff": "A tariff raises the domestic price of an imported good relative to the world price when the importing country is small and competitive. Consumers lose, domestic producers gain and the government collects revenue; the remaining loss is deadweight loss. For a large country, a tariff can improve the terms of trade by lowering the foreign export price, creating a theoretical optimal-tariff argument, but retaliation can erase the gain. With global value chains, tariffs also tax imported inputs used by domestic exporters, so protection for one sector can raise costs for another. Incidence depends on exchange-rate movements, markups and the elasticities of foreign supply and domestic demand.",
    "econ-quota": "An import quota restricts quantity rather than taxing each imported unit. If the quota binds, it raises the domestic price much like a tariff, but the resulting scarcity rents accrue to whoever holds import rights. If licenses are auctioned, the government can capture the rent; if they are allocated freely or to foreign exporters, the distribution differs. Quotas can be more distortionary because they sever the automatic link between demand increases and import quantity. Under imperfect competition they may also strengthen domestic market power. Comparing quotas with tariffs therefore requires tracking not only prices and quantities but who receives the quota rent.",
    "econ-exchange-rate": "An exchange rate is the relative price of two currencies and is simultaneously an asset price. In the short run it responds strongly to interest-rate expectations, risk premia and portfolio flows; in the long run relative price levels and productivity matter. Uncovered interest parity links expected currency changes to interest differentials, but the relationship performs imperfectly empirically because risk premia vary. A depreciation makes foreign goods more expensive in domestic currency and can improve net exports, but the effect depends on invoicing currency, import content and elasticities. Exchange rates therefore transmit both monetary policy and global financial conditions into domestic inflation and activity.",
    "econ-currency-appreciation": "A currency appreciation lowers the domestic-currency price of foreign goods and raises the foreign-currency price of domestic goods, all else equal. That tends to reduce imported inflation and weaken net exports, but pass-through is incomplete because firms adjust markups and many contracts are invoiced in dominant currencies. Exporters that use imported inputs may benefit from cheaper components, partially offsetting the loss of price competitiveness. Financial balance sheets also matter: if firms have foreign-currency debt, appreciation can reduce the domestic value of liabilities. The macro effect is therefore a combination of trade, inflation and balance-sheet channels rather than a simple export story.",
    "econ-current-account": "The current account records trade in goods and services plus primary and secondary income flows. In national accounting it equals national saving minus domestic investment. A deficit therefore means a country is investing more than it saves domestically and is financed by net capital inflows; a surplus is the reverse. This identity does not tell us whether a deficit is good or bad. Borrowing to finance productive investment differs from borrowing to sustain consumption or a housing boom. Exchange rates, fiscal policy, demographics and global asset demand all affect saving and investment. Bilateral trade balances are especially poor welfare metrics because production networks are multilateral.",
    "econ-capital-flows": "International capital flows allocate saving across borders but create exposure to sudden changes in global risk appetite. Foreign direct investment is typically more stable than short-term debt because it embeds long-horizon ownership and operational assets. Portfolio debt can reverse quickly when exchange-rate or refinancing risk rises. Gross positions matter as much as net flows: a country can have a small net position but enormous offsetting foreign assets and liabilities, creating financial fragility. Capital controls, macroprudential regulation and foreign-exchange reserves are policy responses to these risks. The relevant question is not simply how much capital enters, but in what form, currency and maturity.",
    "econ-impossible-trinity": "The impossible trinity states that a country cannot simultaneously maintain a fixed exchange rate, free cross-border capital mobility and independent monetary policy. With open capital markets and a fixed exchange rate, domestic interest rates must stay sufficiently aligned with the anchor country's rates to prevent arbitrage and reserve loss. A country that wants monetary independence must either allow the exchange rate to move or restrict capital mobility. The result is a constraint, not a claim that regimes fall into perfectly discrete boxes. Managed floats, partial controls and macroprudential measures occupy intermediate positions, but the underlying tradeoff remains.",
    "econ-currency-crisis": "A currency crisis occurs when confidence in an exchange-rate regime collapses and authorities cannot or will not defend the currency at the existing rate. First-generation models emphasize inconsistent fiscal or monetary policy that depletes reserves; second-generation models allow self-fulfilling attacks when defending the peg becomes politically costly; third-generation models add bank and corporate foreign-currency balance sheets. The same depreciation can be expansionary through exports or contractionary through debt burdens, depending on currency mismatch. Reserve adequacy, short-term external debt and credibility therefore matter alongside trade fundamentals.",
    "econ-global-value-chains": "Global value chains split production across borders so a final export contains imported intermediate inputs and domestic value added from multiple economies. Gross trade statistics can therefore double-count components that cross borders several times. Tariffs on intermediates act like taxes on domestic downstream production, and exchange-rate effects depend on the currency of invoicing and the share of imported inputs. Supply-chain resilience also involves a tradeoff between cost efficiency and redundancy. Firm-level network structure matters because a disruption at a highly central supplier can propagate far beyond its direct customers. Modern trade analysis increasingly uses value-added rather than gross flows to understand exposure.",
    "econ-economic-growth": "Long-run economic growth can be decomposed into capital deepening, labor growth and productivity. In the Solow model, diminishing returns mean a higher saving rate raises the steady-state level of output per worker but not the permanent growth rate; sustained per-capita growth requires technological progress. Endogenous-growth models make innovation, knowledge spillovers or human capital part of the growth mechanism. Institutions affect whether resources flow toward productive uses, while demographics influence labor supply and saving. Growth rates also compound, so small persistent productivity differences create enormous long-run income gaps. Distinguishing transitional growth from a change in the underlying trend is essential.",
    "econ-human-capital": "Human capital includes skills, knowledge and health that raise productive capacity. Education can increase wages because it raises productivity, because it signals underlying ability, or both. Empirical work therefore uses natural experiments such as compulsory-schooling reforms to identify causal returns. Aggregate effects need not equal private returns if education generates spillovers or merely reallocates workers across jobs. Quality matters as much as years of schooling: test scores and actual competencies often predict growth better than enrollment alone. Human capital also depreciates when skills become obsolete, making continuous learning important in rapidly changing technologies.",
    "econ-institutions-growth": "Institutions affect growth by shaping expected returns to investment, innovation and exchange. Secure property rights, predictable enforcement and constraints on arbitrary expropriation can lengthen investment horizons. But causality runs both ways: richer societies can demand and finance stronger institutions. Historical instruments and natural experiments have been used to identify effects, but these strategies are contested because the exclusion restrictions are difficult. Institutional quality is also multidimensional; a country can have strong bureaucratic capacity and weak political constraints, or vice versa. The most useful analysis links a particular institution to a concrete investment or allocation mechanism rather than treating institutions as a single latent score.",
    "econ-state-capacity": "State capacity is the ability to raise revenue, enforce rules, provide public goods and implement policy across territory. It is complementary to markets because contracts, infrastructure and basic security require organizational capacity. Building capacity often involves fixed costs and political bargaining: rulers invest more in taxation and administration when they expect future revenue or face external threats. Weak capacity can create a low-revenue trap where the state cannot finance the institutions needed to expand the tax base. High capacity is not synonymous with good policy; a capable state can implement both productive and predatory policies. The analytical distinction is between ability and objectives.",
    "econ-poverty-trap": "A poverty trap exists when low income itself causes choices or constraints that keep future income low, generating multiple steady states. Mechanisms can include fixed costs of education, undernutrition, lack of collateral, coordination failures or infrastructure thresholds. The concept requires nonlinearity: if every small investment yields proportional returns, poor households may converge slowly but are not trapped. Empirically, demonstrating a trap is harder than showing poverty persistence. A one-time transfer that permanently moves households to a higher trajectory is stronger evidence than a temporary income gain. Policy relevance therefore depends on identifying the specific threshold mechanism.",
    "econ-structural-transformation": "Structural transformation is the reallocation of labor and output from agriculture toward manufacturing and services as economies develop. Productivity can rise both within sectors and through movement from low-productivity to high-productivity activities. Urbanization, education, infrastructure and firm growth are therefore integral to development. The process is not mechanically manufacturing-led: modern economies may move directly into tradable services, but productivity and scalability differ by sector. Premature deindustrialization can limit a historically important ladder of mass employment. Analysis should separate sector shares driven by relative prices from genuine productivity reallocation.",
    "econ-demographic-transition": "The demographic transition describes the shift from high mortality and fertility to low mortality and fertility. Mortality usually falls first, producing rapid population growth; fertility later declines as child survival improves, urbanization raises child-rearing costs, female education expands and returns to human capital rise. The transition changes age structure, creating a potential demographic dividend when the working-age share rises. That dividend is not automatic: employment, education and institutions determine whether the larger workforce is productive. Later aging reverses the dependency pattern and pressures pension and health systems, making demography a long-run fiscal variable as well as a population phenomenon.",
    "econ-middle-income-trap": "The middle-income trap is the idea that countries can grow rapidly through capital accumulation and labor reallocation but struggle once those easy gains are exhausted. At higher income levels, growth depends more on innovation, advanced skills, firm upgrading and institutions that support creative destruction. The concept is empirically debated because there is no universally accepted income threshold or statistical trap. Some apparent stagnation may simply reflect normal regression toward slower growth as countries converge. The useful question is not whether a country has entered a named trap, but whether its growth model is still compatible with its wage level and technological capabilities.",
    "econ-resource-curse": "Resource abundance can weaken development through several channels: exchange-rate appreciation can hurt tradable sectors, volatile revenues destabilize budgets, concentrated rents intensify political competition, and easy revenue can reduce incentives to build broad tax systems. None of these mechanisms is inevitable. Countries with strong fiscal institutions, transparent ownership rules and stabilization funds can convert resource rents into public assets. The distinction between resource abundance and resource dependence is important; a diversified high-income economy can possess large resources without being dominated by them. Empirical analysis must therefore condition on institutions, price cycles and how rents are managed.",
    "econ-dutch-disease": "Dutch disease occurs when a resource boom or other large foreign-currency inflow appreciates the real exchange rate and shifts resources away from other tradable sectors. The appreciation can occur through a stronger nominal currency or faster domestic price growth. A spending effect raises demand for nontradables, while a resource-movement effect pulls labor and capital toward the booming sector. The result is not automatically harmful: moving resources toward a genuinely more productive sector can be efficient. Concern is greater when learning-by-doing or diversification externalities make the shrinking tradable sector valuable for future productivity. Fiscal sterilization and sovereign wealth funds can moderate the domestic demand surge.",
    "econ-microfinance": "Microfinance relaxes credit constraints for households and small firms that lack collateral or formal banking access. Group lending, dynamic incentives and frequent repayment can substitute partly for conventional collateral. Early claims that microcredit would broadly transform poverty have not been supported by average experimental results; effects on business investment and consumption are typically heterogeneous and modest. That does not imply the product is useless. Credit can smooth cash flow and expand some firms, but debt can also burden borrowers with low-return opportunities. The development question is therefore which clients face profitable but finance-constrained investments, rather than whether credit is universally beneficial.",
    "econ-randomized-development": "Randomized controlled trials identify causal effects by creating treatment and control groups that are comparable in expectation. They have transformed development economics by testing interventions such as information, health products, credit and education. Internal validity can be strong, but external validity is not automatic: an intervention that works in one region may depend on local prices, institutions or implementation quality. Scaling can also generate general-equilibrium effects absent in a small trial. RCTs are therefore best viewed as one tool in a broader causal toolkit. Mechanism evidence and theory help determine when results can travel beyond the original experimental population.",
    "econ-income-inequality": "Income inequality concerns the distribution of flows such as wages, business income, capital income and transfers over a period. It can be measured before taxes, after taxes and transfers, at the household or individual level, and over annual or lifetime horizons; these choices change the result. Market inequality reflects skill prices, technology, institutions, bargaining power and capital ownership. Disposable-income inequality additionally depends on fiscal systems. Inequality can affect macro outcomes through saving, political influence, credit access and human-capital investment, but causal directions are contested. A complete analysis separates descriptive distribution from normative judgments about fairness.",
    "econ-wealth-inequality": "Wealth is a stock of assets minus liabilities, so wealth inequality can be much larger than income inequality because returns compound and asset ownership is concentrated. Housing, pensions, private businesses and financial assets must be valued consistently, while debt can make net wealth negative for some households. Wealth dynamics depend on saving rates, inheritances, asset returns and capital gains. Because rich households hold riskier assets, differential returns can amplify inequality during booms but also expose them to larger market losses. Measurement is difficult at the top because surveys under-sample the very wealthy, which is why administrative and rich-list data are often combined with household surveys.",
    "econ-gini": "The Gini coefficient summarizes inequality using the area between the Lorenz curve and perfect equality. It ranges from zero under complete equality to one under maximal concentration in the idealized limit. The statistic is compact but loses information: two distributions with the same Gini can differ substantially in poverty, top concentration or middle-class outcomes. It is also sensitive to the unit of analysis and whether incomes are equivalized for household size. Cross-country comparisons should use consistent definitions of disposable versus market income. The Gini is therefore a descriptive index, not a welfare measure unless explicit social-welfare assumptions are added.",
    "econ-labor-supply": "Labor supply is a choice between consumption financed by earnings and nonmarket time. A higher wage has a substitution effect that makes leisure more expensive and an income effect that can increase desired leisure, so total hours need not rise. Participation decisions often respond more strongly than hours among already employed workers. Taxes, childcare costs, household bargaining and benefit phase-outs create nonlinear budget constraints. Labor-supply elasticities vary across demographic groups and time horizons, which makes them central to tax-policy analysis. Aggregate labor supply also depends on immigration, retirement and participation, not merely individual weekly hours.",
    "econ-human-capital-wage": "The wage return to education reflects a mixture of productivity enhancement, selection and signaling. A raw wage gap between graduates and nongraduates overstates causal returns if more able or advantaged people obtain more schooling. Quasi-experimental research uses policy changes, admission cutoffs and geographic access to isolate exogenous education variation. Returns also vary by field, institution quality and labor-market conditions. From a social perspective, the relevant return includes spillovers and public costs, not only private wages. Credential inflation can raise required schooling without equivalent productivity gains, so years of education should not be treated as a sufficient statistic for human capital.",
    "econ-minimum-wage": "Minimum-wage effects depend on the structure of the labor market. In a competitive model, a binding floor reduces employment because firms move up their labor-demand curve. In a monopsonistic or search-friction model, employers have wage-setting power, so a moderate minimum can raise wages with little employment loss and can even increase employment over some range. Empirical estimates therefore vary by context, level of the minimum, affected worker share and adjustment channel. Firms may respond through prices, hours, turnover, productivity or profits rather than headcount alone. The relevant policy question is the full distribution of these margins, not only a single employment elasticity.",
    "econ-monopsony": "Labor monopsony does not require a town with one employer. Any friction that makes workers face costs of switching jobs can give firms upward-sloping labor supply. Search costs, geographic constraints, noncompete clauses and differentiated job amenities can all create employer wage-setting power. A monopsonist hires fewer workers and pays lower wages than a competitive market would. This changes policy analysis: minimum wages, unions or pay-transparency rules can reduce the wage wedge without necessarily reducing employment. Measuring monopsony increasingly uses firm-specific labor-supply elasticities and wage responses to worker flows rather than counting employers in a geographic market.",
    "econ-unions": "Unions bargain over wages, benefits, work rules and sometimes employment. Their effects depend on bargaining structure and product-market competition. Higher negotiated wages can reduce employment if firms face elastic product demand, but unions can also reduce turnover, improve communication and increase worker voice. Sectoral bargaining can internalize some competitive pressures differently from firm-level bargaining. Distributionally, unions compress wages among covered workers and can influence nonunion standards. The macro effect depends on coverage, productivity and how wage gains are shared between prices, profits and employment. Treating unions as a simple monopoly supplier of labor misses these organizational channels.",
    "econ-efficiency-wage": "Efficiency-wage models explain why firms may voluntarily pay above the market-clearing wage. Higher pay can reduce turnover, improve applicant quality, raise morale or make job loss more costly, strengthening effort incentives when monitoring is imperfect. If many firms adopt such wages, involuntary unemployment can persist because cutting wages would reduce productivity or discipline. This provides a microfoundation for wage rigidity and unemployment without assuming workers irrationally refuse lower pay. The theory predicts that wage differences can reflect monitoring and turnover conditions rather than only worker productivity. Empirical relevance varies across occupations and institutions.",
    "econ-rent-seeking": "Economic rent is a return above the minimum needed to keep a resource in its current use. Rent seeking is expenditure aimed at obtaining or protecting such transfers rather than creating new surplus. Lobbying for exclusive licenses, barriers to entry or favorable quotas can dissipate part of the rent through competition to capture it. The social cost therefore exceeds the transfer itself when resources are consumed in the contest. Not all lobbying is rent seeking; providing information or advocating genuine public goods can improve policy. The analytical test is whether effort primarily changes the distribution of existing surplus or expands total value.",
    "econ-regulatory-capture": "Regulatory capture occurs when agencies systematically serve regulated interests rather than the broader public objective they were created to pursue. Mechanisms include concentrated industry information, revolving-door careers, lobbying and asymmetry between highly organized firms and diffuse consumers. Capture is not the only explanation for industry-friendly regulation; technical constraints or legitimate cost-benefit tradeoffs can produce similar outcomes. Empirical evidence therefore requires tracing incentives and decision processes. Institutional responses include transparency, conflict-of-interest rules, independent expertise and multiple centers of review, but these can add bureaucracy. The problem is an application of principal-agent theory to public institutions.",
    "econ-collective-action-econ": "Collective action problems arise when a group would benefit from a common outcome but each member has an incentive to free ride on others' contributions. Large groups are especially difficult to organize when individual stakes are small and monitoring is costly. Selective incentives, repeated interaction, identity and institutional rules can overcome the problem. The theory helps explain why small producer groups can sometimes organize more effectively than millions of consumers even when the aggregate consumer stake is larger. It also predicts that political organization depends on the concentration of benefits and costs, not simply on the total size of those benefits and costs.",
    "econ-median-voter": "The median voter theorem states that under restrictive conditions, two vote-maximizing parties choosing positions on a one-dimensional policy line converge toward the median voter's ideal point. Those conditions include single-peaked preferences, majority rule and essentially two competitors. Real politics is multidimensional, turnout is endogenous, parties have activists and candidates may value policy as well as office. The theorem is therefore a benchmark for understanding centripetal incentives, not a universal law of elections. It also says nothing about whether the median outcome is economically efficient or normatively desirable.",
    "econ-concentrated-benefits": "Policies with concentrated benefits and diffuse costs can be politically durable because beneficiaries have strong incentives to organize while each payer loses too little to invest heavily in opposition. This is an information and collective-action mechanism, not a claim that all concentrated-benefit policies are inefficient. A tariff protecting a small industry, for example, can generate visible gains for producers while spreading consumer costs across millions of purchases. The model predicts asymmetry in lobbying intensity and attention. Institutional design can counter it through transparency, broad budget rules or competition between interest groups, but no mechanism removes political incentives entirely.",
    "econ-great-depression": "The Great Depression combined a collapse in demand, banking crises, debt deflation and policy constraints under the interwar gold standard. The U.S. money supply contracted sharply as banks failed and the Federal Reserve did not fully offset the decline. Falling prices raised real debt burdens, weakening borrowers and banks. Internationally, gold-standard commitments transmitted deflation and limited monetary easing; countries that left gold earlier generally recovered earlier. Fiscal policy was modest by later standards until wartime mobilization. The episode is important because no single explanation is sufficient: monetary contraction, financial fragility, expectations and international regime constraints interacted to deepen and prolong the downturn.",
    "econ-gold-standard": "Under a gold standard, currencies are convertible into gold at fixed parities, tying domestic monetary conditions to gold flows and confidence in convertibility. Adjustment to external deficits can require domestic deflation and higher interest rates, especially when reserve losses threaten the peg. The system can provide a nominal anchor but reduces monetary-policy autonomy and can transmit shocks internationally. Credibility depends on whether governments are willing to prioritize convertibility over employment or banking stability. The interwar gold standard was particularly fragile because political and financial conditions differed from the prewar regime. The analytical tradeoff resembles a hard version of the open-economy trilemma.",
    "econ-bretton-woods": "Bretton Woods created a system of fixed but adjustable exchange rates centered on the U.S. dollar, which was convertible into gold for official holders. Capital controls gave countries more monetary-policy autonomy than under fully open capital markets. The IMF was created to support balance-of-payments adjustment. The system became strained as global demand for dollar reserves grew while U.S. gold backing became less credible, a tension often associated with the Triffin dilemma. The United States suspended gold convertibility in 1971, and major currencies moved toward floating rates. The episode shows how reserve-currency systems combine domestic policy with international liquidity provision.",
    "econ-stagflation-70s": "The 1970s stagflation episode reflected repeated oil shocks, accommodative policy, wage-setting institutions and inflation expectations that became less anchored. The experience weakened simple versions of the Phillips curve that treated the inflation-unemployment tradeoff as stable. Once workers and firms incorporate expected inflation into wage and price decisions, maintaining unemployment below its equilibrium rate requires continuously accelerating inflation. The period also highlighted supply-side constraints because higher energy costs reduced real income and potential output. Disinflation later required sustained monetary tightening and significant output losses, illustrating how costly it can be to re-anchor expectations after credibility deteriorates.",
    "econ-volcker-shock": "The Volcker disinflation involved a sharp tightening of U.S. monetary policy beginning in 1979 to break persistent high inflation. Short-term interest rates rose dramatically, contributing to deep recessions in the early 1980s and high unemployment. Inflation subsequently fell and long-run inflation expectations became better anchored. The episode is central to debates about the sacrifice ratio: how much lost output is required to reduce inflation when expectations have become embedded. It also had international effects by strengthening the dollar and raising debt-service burdens for countries with dollar liabilities. The case illustrates both the power and the real economic cost of restoring monetary credibility.",
    "econ-asian-crisis": "The Asian financial crisis combined fixed or managed exchange rates, short-term foreign-currency borrowing, leveraged domestic financial systems and sudden capital-flow reversals. When confidence fell, currency depreciation increased the domestic-currency value of foreign debts, damaging banks and firms and deepening recession. This balance-sheet channel made devaluation contractionary despite improved export competitiveness. The crisis also exposed weaknesses in financial supervision and maturity management. Policy responses remain debated, including the timing of fiscal tightening and capital controls. The broader lesson is that currency and banking crises can reinforce each other when private balance sheets contain large unhedged foreign-currency liabilities.",
    "econ-dotcom": "The dot-com bubble combined genuine technological innovation with extreme uncertainty about future cash flows and business models. Equity valuations rose rapidly for internet-related firms, many with little revenue, before collapsing after 2000. A bubble interpretation emphasizes prices exceeding fundamentals through extrapolation, speculative demand or agency problems, but fundamentals were unusually difficult to estimate because a general-purpose technology was creating new markets. The crash destroyed substantial market value yet did not eliminate the underlying internet transformation. This distinction is important: an investment boom can overprice firms while still financing infrastructure and experimentation that produces lasting productivity benefits.",
    "econ-global-financial-crisis": "The global financial crisis grew from a leveraged housing and credit boom, weak underwriting, securitization chains and heavy reliance on short-term wholesale funding. Falling U.S. house prices created losses on mortgage-linked assets, but the systemic crisis came from opacity, leverage and interconnected balance sheets. Institutions that appeared well capitalized under normal assumptions faced runs in repo and money-market funding. Fire sales reduced asset prices, causing mark-to-market losses and further deleveraging. Policy responses included emergency liquidity, bank recapitalization, guarantees, fiscal stimulus and unconventional monetary policy. The episode shifted regulation toward higher capital, liquidity buffers and resolution planning.",
    "econ-euro-crisis": "The euro-area sovereign-debt crisis exposed the tension between a common currency and nationally fragmented fiscal and banking systems. Member states could not devalue or issue currency independently, and banks held large quantities of domestic sovereign debt, creating a doom loop between bank solvency and government finances. Sudden stops in cross-border funding produced severe adjustment in peripheral economies. ECB interventions, including later commitments to preserve monetary transmission, reduced redenomination risk. Institutional reforms added banking supervision and rescue mechanisms, but fiscal integration remained limited. The episode is a case study in incomplete monetary union rather than simply excessive public debt.",
    "econ-pandemic-economics": "The pandemic shock simultaneously restricted supply and collapsed demand in contact-intensive sectors. Governments used transfers, wage subsidies, credit guarantees and monetary easing to prevent temporary shutdowns from becoming mass insolvency and unemployment. The recovery then interacted with supply bottlenecks, shifts from services toward goods, labor-market reallocation, energy shocks and extraordinary fiscal support, contributing to a surge in inflation. The episode illustrates why standard demand-management models must incorporate sectoral constraints. It also highlights option value in crisis policy: preserving viable firms and worker matches can reduce scarring even when precise targeting is impossible in real time.",
    "econ-bounded-rationality": "Bounded rationality recognizes that optimization is costly because attention, memory and computation are limited. Simon's satisficing framework suggests agents search until they find an acceptable option rather than globally optimizing. Modern models make the limitation explicit through rational inattention, sparse attention or heuristic decision rules. This can change market equilibrium because firms may exploit inattentive consumers through complex fees or shrouded attributes. The concept should not become a residual explanation for any mistake; a useful model specifies which information is costly, what heuristic is used and how behavior changes when stakes or experience increase.",
    "econ-loss-aversion-econ": "Loss aversion means outcomes below a reference point receive greater marginal weight than equal-sized gains above it. In prospect theory the value function is kinked around that reference point and typically concave for gains and convex for losses. The key empirical difficulty is determining the reference point: status quo, expectations and recent purchase price can generate different predictions. Loss aversion has been used to explain endowment effects, reluctance to realize investment losses and labor-supply patterns. But some apparent effects can be produced by transaction costs or beliefs, so careful designs distinguish reference dependence from alternative mechanisms.",
    "econ-present-bias": "Present bias gives disproportionate weight to immediate gratification relative to future tradeoffs. A quasi-hyperbolic model represents this with a beta parameter that discounts all future periods relative to the present, in addition to ordinary exponential discounting. The result is time inconsistency: today's self plans to save, exercise or repay tomorrow, but tomorrow's self revises the plan. Sophisticated agents anticipate this and may demand commitment devices; naive agents do not. The model has implications for retirement saving, consumer credit and addiction. Policy analysis depends on whether observed choices reflect stable preferences or self-control failures.",
    "econ-nudge": "A nudge changes choice architecture without substantially changing prices or restricting options. Defaults, reminders, simplification and framing can alter behavior when attention or inertia matters. The important economic question is effect size and persistence, not whether an intervention sounds behaviorally informed. Defaults can be powerful in pension enrollment, but their welfare effect depends on whether the default is reasonably matched to heterogeneous preferences. Firms can also use choice architecture strategically, creating sludge rather than helpful nudges. Behavioral public policy therefore requires auditing both government and private interfaces and measuring outcomes beyond immediate take-up.",
    "econ-default-econ": "Defaults matter when switching requires attention, effort or psychological commitment. Automatic enrollment can sharply increase participation because many people accept the preselected option. A default effect does not by itself prove irrationality; accepting the default may be sensible when the designer is believed to have information or when switching costs are real. Welfare analysis therefore distinguishes endorsement, inertia and information channels. Good design also considers heterogeneity: a single default can be too conservative for some users and too aggressive for others. Easy opt-out and adaptive defaults can reduce this problem.",
    "econ-information-cascade": "An information cascade occurs when people rationally infer so much from previous actions that they ignore their own private signals. Once a cascade starts, public behavior can become disconnected from the amount of underlying information because later actions reveal little new private information. This makes cascades fragile: a small public signal can overturn them. Financial herding, product adoption and social behavior can all exhibit this logic, though shared fundamentals or imitation costs can produce similar patterns. The model shows how individually rational Bayesian behavior can generate poor aggregate information aggregation without requiring irrational crowd psychology.",
    "econ-signaling": "Signaling occurs when an informed party takes a costly action that reveals type because the action is differentially costly across types. Spence's education model is the classic example: education can raise wages partly by signaling productivity even if it does not itself increase productivity. A separating equilibrium requires incentive compatibility so low types do not imitate high types. Signals can be socially wasteful if they consume resources without improving output, yet privately rational because they affect beliefs. In markets, warranties, collateral and certifications can serve analogous roles. The analytical question is what makes the signal credible rather than merely visible.",
    "econ-screening": "Screening is the uninformed party's attempt to induce informed types to reveal themselves through a menu of choices. Insurance contracts can pair low premiums with high deductibles and high premiums with low deductibles, causing different risk types to self-select. The resulting allocation generally differs from full-information efficiency because contract terms are distorted to maintain incentive compatibility. Screening appears in credit, employment, procurement and pricing. The key distinction from signaling is who moves first: the uninformed side designs the menu. Mechanism design generalizes this idea by constructing rules that make truthful revelation compatible with private incentives.",
    "econ-winner-curse": "The winner's curse arises in common-value auctions where the asset has roughly the same underlying value to everyone but bidders receive noisy private estimates. The highest bidder is likely to have the most optimistic error, so naive bidding tends to overpay conditional on winning. Rational bidders shade bids downward to account for this selection effect. The curse is stronger when uncertainty and the number of bidders are large. Oil leases, acquisitions and procurement can exhibit analogous logic. Experienced participants may adapt, which is why the concept is about conditional inference rather than an unavoidable psychological mistake.",
    "econ-market-design": "Market design treats rules as variables rather than taking the market institution as given. Matching markets such as school choice or medical residency may not use prices, so mechanisms must elicit preferences and create stable assignments. Auction design chooses information, bidding and allocation rules to balance revenue, efficiency and incentives. A central objective is strategy-proofness or at least reducing gains from manipulation. Design also confronts practical constraints such as congestion, fairness requirements and limited information. The field is engineering-oriented economics: it uses game theory and empirical evidence to build institutions whose equilibrium behavior produces desired allocation properties.",
    "econ-effective-demand": "Effective demand is the level of aggregate expenditure that firms expect to translate into sales and therefore into production and employment. In Keynes's argument, there is no guarantee that this demand corresponds to full employment. Firms can collectively plan production consistent with lower employment because households' saving intentions and firms' investment intentions need not generate sufficient spending. The concept differs from a simple preference for consuming more; investment demand can also collapse when expected returns fall. Modern models represent the mechanism through output gaps and nominal rigidities, but the conceptual point remains that desired spending can constrain realized production.",
    "econ-paradox-thrift": "The paradox of thrift arises when individually prudent attempts to save more reduce aggregate income enough that realized saving does not rise by the intended amount. The mechanism requires demand-constrained output: lower consumption reduces firms' revenues, leading to lower production and income. In a full-employment economy with flexible adjustment, higher saving can instead finance investment and raise future capital. The paradox is therefore state-dependent, not a universal argument against saving. Monetary policy, investment sensitivity and foreign demand determine how much the fall in consumption is offset. It is a classic example of a fallacy of composition in macroeconomics.",
    "econ-liquidity-preference": "Liquidity preference treats the interest rate as partly the return required to persuade wealth holders to give up liquid money for less liquid assets. Keynes distinguished transaction, precautionary and speculative motives for holding money. In modern terms, safe and liquid assets provide convenience yield beyond pecuniary return. During stress, demand for liquidity can rise sharply, compressing safe yields and widening spreads on risky assets. Central-bank liquidity provision can therefore affect more than the money stock: it changes the supply of assets that satisfy settlement and safety demands. The concept connects monetary policy to portfolio choice and financial stability.",
    "econ-animal-spirits": "Animal spirits refers to the role of confidence and judgment when investment decisions cannot be derived from known probabilities. Long-lived projects depend on beliefs about distant future demand, technology and policy, so small changes in confidence can produce large investment swings. Modern macroeconomics often formalizes similar effects through uncertainty shocks, news shocks or belief-driven fluctuations rather than using the original terminology. The important distinction is between measurable risk and fundamental uncertainty. If probabilities themselves are unstable or unknown, market prices may not aggregate a fixed distribution of outcomes, making conventions and narratives economically relevant.",
    "econ-underemployment-equilibrium": "An underemployment equilibrium is a state in which planned expenditure is consistent with output below full-employment capacity. Firms will not hire simply because unemployed labor is available if expected demand does not justify additional production. Wage cuts need not solve the problem because they reduce worker income, can raise real debt burdens under deflation and may leave aggregate demand weaker. Modern models do not usually use Keynes's exact equilibrium construction, but sticky prices and demand shortfalls generate analogous output gaps. The concept attacks the assumption that any unemployment automatically creates a price adjustment that restores full employment quickly.",
    "econ-marginal-efficiency-capital": "The marginal efficiency of capital is the discount rate that equates an investment project's purchase price with the present value of its expected future returns. Investment rises when this expected return exceeds the financing cost, but expectations can move independently of current interest rates. Keynes used the concept to emphasize the volatility of investment: long-lived capital projects rely on uncertain forecasts, so confidence shifts can move the schedule sharply. Modern corporate finance would express the same decision through net present value and risk-adjusted discount rates. The macroeconomic importance comes from investment's volatility and multiplier effects on income.",
    "econ-sticky-prices": "Sticky prices mean firms do not adjust prices continuously in response to shocks. Reasons include menu costs, customer relationships, contracts, information frictions and strategic complementarity. In Calvo-style New Keynesian models only a fraction of firms can reprice each period, so monetary-policy changes alter real demand before the overall price level adjusts. The resulting Phillips curve links current inflation to expected future inflation and real marginal cost. Price rigidity is heterogeneous across sectors, which affects transmission. Sticky prices do not imply prices never move; they imply adjustment is staggered enough that nominal shocks have temporary real effects.",
    "econ-automatic-stabilizers": "Automatic stabilizers change taxes and transfers with economic conditions without new legislative action. In a downturn, income-tax receipts fall and unemployment-related transfers rise, supporting disposable income and demand; in a boom the reverse occurs. Because they respond immediately, they avoid some decision and implementation lags of discretionary fiscal policy. Their strength depends on tax progressivity, benefit systems and the size of government. Automatic stabilizers also make headline budget deficits partly cyclical, so analysts distinguish structural from cyclical fiscal balances. They stabilize demand but do not eliminate the need for discretionary responses to unusually large shocks.",
    "econ-liquidity-trap": "A liquidity trap occurs when conventional monetary easing loses traction because short-term nominal rates are near their effective lower bound and money-like assets become close substitutes for short government securities. Increasing reserves then may have little additional effect on short rates. Expectations about future policy, long-term asset purchases and fiscal policy become more important. In modern New Keynesian language, the deeper problem is that the natural real rate can fall below the lowest feasible nominal rate, making the real policy stance too tight. Forward guidance and QE attempt to influence longer-term real rates even when the overnight rate cannot be reduced much further.",
    "econ-keynes-vs-hayek": "The Keynes-Hayek debate is often caricatured as spending versus austerity, but the deeper disagreement concerns coordination across time. Keynes emphasized unstable investment expectations, deficient aggregate demand and the possibility of persistent unemployment. Hayek emphasized relative-price signals, capital structure and the danger that credit conditions can induce investment patterns inconsistent with underlying intertemporal preferences. Modern macroeconomics incorporates pieces of both traditions: demand shortfalls and nominal rigidities matter, but so do financial imbalances and misallocation. The empirical question is which mechanism dominates in a particular episode. Treating the debate as a permanent policy binary obscures that state dependence.",
    "econ-labor-theory-value-marx": "Marx's value theory uses socially necessary labor time as an organizing principle for analyzing capitalist production and the distribution of surplus. It is not simply a claim that market prices equal embodied labor hours at every moment. Marx distinguishes values from prices of production and analyzes how competition redistributes surplus across sectors with different capital intensities. This creates the famous transformation problem and a large interpretive literature. Mainstream economics instead explains relative prices through marginal preferences and production constraints. Comparing the frameworks requires care because they ask somewhat different questions: Marx uses value categories to analyze social relations and accumulation, not only observed price ratios.",
    "econ-commodity": "For Marx, a commodity has both use value and exchange value and is produced for exchange rather than direct use. The commodity form matters because social coordination occurs through market exchange: producers confront one another through the prices of things rather than through direct planning of social labor. This is the starting point for Marx's analysis of money, value and capital. A commodity is therefore not merely any physical object sold in a market; services can also be commodified, while goods produced for household use are not commodities in the same analytical sense. The category is relational and institutional.",
    "econ-commodity-fetishism": "Commodity fetishism describes how social relations among producers appear as relations among commodities and prices. Market exchange makes value seem like a property naturally residing in objects, while the underlying relations of production and dependence become less visible. The concept is not simply consumer obsession with brands. It is a theory of how market forms shape perception: coordination through exchange presents social labor in impersonal price signals. One can accept the descriptive insight about depersonalized coordination without accepting the full Marxian value framework. The analytical question is what information and social relations are obscured when economic interaction is represented only through prices.",
    "econ-alienation-marx": "Marx's early theory of alienation describes estrangement from the product of labor, the labor process, other people and human capacities under wage labor organized by external control. It is not equivalent to dissatisfaction or burnout, though those experiences can overlap. The economic mechanism is tied to ownership and control: workers sell labor power and produce under objectives they do not determine, while the product belongs to the employer. Later Marx focused less on the philosophical language of alienation and more on exploitation and accumulation. Modern labor economics addresses related issues through job control, intrinsic motivation and organizational design without adopting Marx's broader anthropological claims.",
    "econ-reserve-army": "The reserve army of labor is Marx's concept for a pool of unemployed or underemployed workers that disciplines wage demands and supports capital accumulation. As firms mechanize or restructure, workers can be displaced, while expansions draw labor back in. The mechanism resembles modern ideas about labor-market slack and bargaining power, although the theoretical frameworks differ. Marx used it to argue that unemployment is not merely an accidental deviation from equilibrium but can be functionally reproduced by capitalist accumulation. Empirical relevance depends on labor institutions, bargaining structures and how technological change affects labor demand.",
    "econ-organic-composition": "The organic composition of capital refers roughly to the relationship between capital invested in means of production and capital advanced for labor power, interpreted through Marx's value categories. Marx expected technological development to raise the capital intensity of production. Because surplus value is generated through labor in his framework, a rising composition creates pressure on the profit rate unless offset by higher exploitation, cheaper capital goods, faster turnover or other counteracting forces. It should not be equated mechanically with the modern capital-labor ratio, since the numerator and denominator are defined differently. The concept matters mainly within Marx's accumulation theory.",
    "econ-falling-profit": "Marx's tendency of the rate of profit to fall follows from the idea that competitive accumulation raises the organic composition of capital while surplus value originates in labor. The tendency is explicitly counteracted by factors such as higher exploitation, cheaper capital goods, foreign trade and devaluation of existing capital. Because both the numerator and denominator are theoretical value categories, empirical tests using national-accounting profits and capital stocks are not straightforward. Modern economists also study falling returns to capital, markups and profitability cycles through different frameworks. The important point is that Marx proposed a tendency with countervailing forces, not a monotonic law that profit rates must always decline.",
    "econ-primitive-accumulation": "Primitive accumulation describes the historical processes that separated producers from direct access to means of subsistence and created both wage labor and concentrated property. Marx discussed enclosure, colonial extraction, state debt and coercion as part of the prehistory of capitalist relations. The concept challenges narratives in which capitalism emerges solely from voluntary saving and exchange. Later scholarship has extended the term to ongoing dispossession, though that broader use is debated. Economically, the key issue is that initial asset distribution and legal institutions can shape who enters markets as an owner, worker or debtor, affecting subsequent bargaining and accumulation.",
    "econ-class-struggle": "Class struggle in Marxian analysis arises because groups occupy different positions in production and have conflicting claims over surplus, control and working conditions. Competition occurs within classes as well as between them, so collective organization is not automatic. Institutions such as unions, parties and labor law affect whether dispersed workers can translate shared interests into bargaining power. In modern political economy, related mechanisms appear in models of factor-based preferences, collective action and distributional conflict. The Marxian framework differs by treating class relations as constitutive of the production system rather than one interest group among many.",
    "econ-marx-crisis": "Marxian crisis theory is not a single mechanism. Interpretations emphasize profitability pressures, overaccumulation, underconsumption, disproportionality between sectors, financial fragility or the contradiction between production and realization of surplus value. Competition can induce firms to expand capacity and leverage even when aggregate demand cannot validate all expected profits. Crisis then devalues capital, bankrupts firms and restructures production. Modern heterodox models often combine these ideas with Keynesian demand and Minskyan finance. Any empirical application should identify which mechanism is claimed to bind, because invoking a generic contradiction of capitalism is too broad to generate testable predictions.",
    "econ-knowledge-problem": "Hayek's knowledge problem concerns information that is dispersed, local and often tacit rather than centrally given. A planner may know engineering relationships but still lack real-time knowledge of local scarcity, preferences and alternative uses. Prices summarize some of this information by rewarding economizing behavior without requiring agents to understand the whole system. The argument does not imply that markets perfectly aggregate all information; externalities, monopoly and strategic manipulation can distort signals. It is a comparative institutional claim: evaluate which system can acquire, communicate and act on the relevant knowledge at acceptable cost.",
    "econ-price-signals-hayek": "In Hayek's account, a price change communicates relative scarcity without transmitting the underlying story. If tin becomes scarcer, users economize and search for substitutes whether the cause was a mine closure or new demand elsewhere. This economizes on information requirements. The signal, however, is only as good as the institutions generating the price. Subsidies, market power, externalities or missing property rights can cause prices to diverge from social opportunity cost. The deeper insight is informational compression: prices coordinate decentralized adjustments, but they do not contain every value-relevant fact, especially when effects fall on people outside the market.",
    "econ-spontaneous-order": "Spontaneous order refers to complex coordination that emerges from decentralized interaction rather than central design. Markets, language and some legal conventions can evolve through repeated local adaptation. The concept does not mean institutions are unplanned in every detail; designed rules and evolved practices can coexist. Nor does emergence guarantee efficiency or justice. Path dependence can preserve inferior conventions, and private incentives can sustain externalities. Hayek's analytical caution is that reformers should understand the information-processing role of existing institutions before replacing them. The challenge is to distinguish adaptive order from mere historical persistence.",
    "econ-austrian-business-cycle": "Austrian business-cycle theory argues that credit expansion can push market interest rates below rates consistent with intertemporal preferences, encouraging overly roundabout investment. The resulting capital structure is unsustainable because planned future consumption and investment are inconsistent with real saving. When credit conditions normalize, projects are revealed as malinvestments and must be liquidated or restructured. Mainstream macroeconomics disputes whether this mechanism is general and whether the natural-rate concept can support the strong sectoral predictions. Empirical evaluation requires showing that credit-driven interest distortions systematically create the predicted pattern of capital misallocation, not merely that booms involve low rates and later recessions.",
    "econ-malinvestment": "Malinvestment is Austrian terminology for capital projects that appear profitable under distorted financing conditions but are inconsistent with underlying resource availability or future demand. Because capital goods are heterogeneous and partly irreversible, correcting the mistake can be costly. The concept resembles broader ideas of credit misallocation and overinvestment, though Austrian theory gives the interest-rate signal a special role. Distinguishing malinvestment from ordinary entrepreneurial failure is difficult ex ante. A project that fails after a downturn may have been sensible given information available at the time. Strong claims therefore need evidence that a systematic price distortion, not random uncertainty, drove the investment pattern.",
    "econ-entrepreneurial-discovery": "Entrepreneurial discovery treats profit opportunities as something found through experimentation rather than known in advance. In Kirzner's formulation, entrepreneurship is alertness to discrepancies in prices and unmet opportunities, and successful action tends to coordinate markets. Schumpeter's entrepreneur is more disruptive, creating new combinations rather than merely noticing existing gaps. Both approaches shift focus from equilibrium allocation to process. The empirical counterpart is entry, product experimentation and reallocation. Institutions matter because bankruptcy law, finance and regulation change the cost of trying and failing, which in turn changes the amount of discovery activity.",
    "econ-subjective-value": "Subjective value means economic value depends on individuals' preferences and circumstances rather than on an objective quantity embodied in the good. The marginal unit matters because the value of an additional unit depends on how urgently it can be used at the margin. This insight helped resolve the diamond-water paradox: water can have enormous total usefulness but low marginal value where it is abundant. Modern consumer theory formalizes subjective valuation through preferences and willingness to pay. Subjectivity does not mean prices are arbitrary; market prices emerge from interaction between valuations, endowments, technology and strategic conditions.",
    "econ-economic-calculation": "The economic-calculation debate asks how alternative uses of heterogeneous capital goods can be compared when market prices are absent or heavily administered. Mises argued that accounting in physical units cannot produce a common opportunity-cost metric for thousands of alternative production plans. Hayek later emphasized that the needed knowledge is itself decentralized and discovered through market interaction. Critics point to large internal planning systems and computational optimization, but firms still rely on external prices for many inputs and outputs. The enduring issue is how institutions generate the data needed for calculation, not merely whether optimization algorithms exist.",
    "econ-rule-law-hayek": "Hayek's rule-of-law ideal emphasizes general, predictable and relatively stable rules rather than discretionary commands targeted at particular outcomes. The economic argument is that predictable rules allow individuals to form plans using local knowledge without needing to anticipate arbitrary intervention. This is distinct from having minimal government; a substantial legal framework can still satisfy generality and predictability. Tensions arise because modern regulation often requires discretion to handle complex risks. The analytical tradeoff is between adaptability and predictability: rigid rules reduce arbitrary power but can respond poorly to unforeseen circumstances.",
    "econ-hayek-competition-discovery": "Hayek treated competition as a discovery process because firms test prices, products and production methods under uncertainty. If all relevant cost and demand information were already known, the main coordination problem would largely be solved before competition began. Entry and failure therefore produce information about what works. This perspective makes barriers to experimentation particularly important. It also cautions against evaluating competition solely by the number of firms at one moment. A market with rapid entry and innovation may be dynamically competitive even when concentration is high, while formal fragmentation can coexist with little real experimentation.",
    "econ-quantity-money": "The quantity equation MV = PY is an identity when velocity is defined as nominal spending divided by the money stock. Quantity theory becomes substantive only with assumptions about how velocity and real output behave. In the long run, if real output is determined by real factors and money demand is stable, faster money growth mainly raises prices. In the short run, velocity can move sharply and output can respond, weakening mechanical proportionality. Financial innovation also changes which monetary aggregate is most relevant. The theory's durable contribution is the link between sustained nominal spending and the monetary regime, not a fixed one-to-one short-run mapping from M2 growth to inflation.",
    "econ-inflation-monetary": "Friedman's statement that inflation is always and everywhere a monetary phenomenon is best interpreted as a claim about sustained aggregate inflation over time, not about every individual price shock. Oil prices, taxes or supply disruptions can raise the price level, but persistent inflation requires nominal demand to continue validating higher prices and wages. Modern central banks implement this control through interest rates and expectations rather than fixed money-supply quantities. Fiscal behavior can also matter if monetary policy is constrained by debt sustainability. The lasting point is that long-run nominal stability requires a monetary-fiscal regime consistent with the inflation target.",
    "econ-permanent-income": "The permanent-income hypothesis says consumption depends more on expected long-run resources than on current measured income. A temporary income windfall should therefore have a smaller consumption effect than a persistent increase. Under certainty-equivalent versions, consumers smooth marginal utility across time using saving and borrowing. Liquidity constraints, precautionary saving and present bias create deviations, which is why low-income households may have much higher marginal propensities to consume from transfers than unconstrained households. The theory remains central because it turns fiscal-policy analysis into a question about persistence, credit access and expectations rather than current income alone.",
    "econ-natural-rate-friedman": "Friedman's natural-rate argument introduced expectations into the inflation-unemployment tradeoff. If policymakers push unemployment below the rate consistent with labor-market structure, workers and firms eventually revise expected inflation upward, shifting the short-run Phillips curve. Maintaining the lower unemployment rate would then require accelerating inflation. This destroyed the idea of a permanent exploitable tradeoff. The natural rate itself can move with demographics, institutions and matching efficiency, so it is not a policy-invariant constant. Modern macro retains the expectations logic while using richer labor-market and price-setting models.",
    "econ-adaptive-expectations": "Adaptive expectations update forecasts gradually using past forecast errors. A simple rule might raise expected inflation when actual inflation exceeds the previous forecast. This can generate persistence because expectations adjust with a lag, allowing temporary policy effects on real activity. Rational-expectations models criticized the approach for ignoring available forward-looking information, but purely model-consistent expectations can also be unrealistic. Contemporary work often uses learning, heterogeneous expectations or information frictions. Adaptive expectations remain useful as a benchmark for understanding how inflation inertia can emerge when agents extrapolate from recent experience.",
    "econ-monetary-rule": "A monetary rule commits policy to a predictable response rather than discretionary fine-tuning. Friedman's historical proposal was a constant money-growth rule, motivated by uncertainty about lags and policymaker behavior. Modern rules are more often interest-rate reaction functions such as the Taylor rule, which raises the policy rate when inflation exceeds target or output is strong. Rules can anchor expectations and reduce time inconsistency, but strict formulas can perform badly under unusual shocks or financial stress. The practical debate is therefore rules versus constrained discretion: how much systematic predictability can be preserved while allowing judgment when the model or shock is outside normal experience.",
    "econ-great-depression-friedman": "Friedman and Anna Schwartz argued that Federal Reserve failures transformed an ordinary contraction into the Great Depression by allowing bank failures and a severe money-supply collapse. Their interpretation challenged views that monetary policy had been powerless because nominal interest rates were low; they emphasized quantities, bank liabilities and real rates under deflation. Later research added gold-standard constraints, debt deflation and international transmission. The modern consensus gives monetary and banking mechanisms a major role without reducing the episode to one cause. The case changed central banking by strengthening the presumption that authorities should prevent systemic liquidity collapse.",
    "econ-floating-rates-friedman": "Friedman favored flexible exchange rates because they allow domestic monetary policy to adjust without defending a fixed parity. Under floating rates, external shocks can be absorbed partly through the currency rather than through domestic wage and price deflation. He also argued that speculation need not destabilize markets if profitable traders buy undervalued currencies and sell overvalued ones. In practice, floating rates can be volatile and many countries manage them, especially when balance sheets contain foreign-currency debt. The core advantage remains policy autonomy under high capital mobility, consistent with the open-economy trilemma.",
    "econ-school-vouchers-friedman": "Friedman's voucher proposal separates public financing of education from public production. Families receive publicly funded purchasing power and choose among eligible schools, with the aim of introducing competition and consumer choice. Economic evaluation focuses on school quality, peer effects, sorting, information, entry and whether competitive pressure improves public schools. Results depend heavily on program design and local institutional context. Distribution also matters because informed or mobile families may use choice systems differently from disadvantaged families. The proposal is analytically important as an application of quasi-market design to a publicly financed service.",
    "econ-negative-income-tax": "A negative income tax guarantees a minimum income and then withdraws support gradually as earnings rise. It can unify cash assistance with the tax system and avoid the sharp benefit cliffs created by categorical programs. The central design parameter is the phase-out rate: a slower phase-out strengthens work incentives but increases fiscal cost, while a faster phase-out saves money but raises the effective marginal tax rate on recipients. Modern earned-income credits and universal basic income debates use related logic. Evaluation therefore requires looking at labor-supply responses, take-up, administrative simplicity and the interaction with other taxes and benefits.",
    "econ-invisible-hand": "Smith's invisible-hand metaphor does not establish a general theorem that private self-interest always maximizes social welfare. In competitive settings, decentralized price adjustment can align private plans, but modern welfare theorems require conditions such as complete markets and no externalities. Smith himself discussed monopoly, public goods and institutional preconditions. The deeper idea is emergent coordination: individuals pursuing local objectives can generate an order no one centrally intended. Economic analysis then asks where the mechanism works and where wedges between private and social costs break it. Treating the phrase as a universal policy rule strips away those conditions.",
    "econ-smith-self-interest": "Smith's account of self-interest is institutional rather than psychologically reductionist. Exchange often relies on appealing to others' interests, but commercial society also depends on trust, justice and norms. In The Theory of Moral Sentiments, Smith analyzes sympathy, impartial judgment and moral approval, showing that he did not model human motivation as pure material selfishness. Economically, this matters because market coordination works through predictable rules and repeated social interaction. Self-interest can support specialization and exchange, but it can also motivate collusion or rent seeking when institutions permit it. The outcome depends on the rules of the game.",
    "econ-smith-moral-sentiments": "The Theory of Moral Sentiments provides the ethical psychology behind Smith's economic analysis. Individuals seek not only material gain but approval, propriety and the ability to view themselves through an impartial spectator. This helps explain why commercial order cannot be reduced to utility maximization under prices. Norms of trust and justice lower transaction costs and constrain opportunism. The so-called Adam Smith problem—the claim that his moral philosophy conflicts with the self-interest of The Wealth of Nations—is largely a false dichotomy. Smith treats motives as plural and institutions as channels that can convert or restrain them.",
    "econ-smith-specialization": "Smith's specialization mechanism has both within-firm and economy-wide dimensions. Repetition increases dexterity, task separation reduces switching costs, and specialization encourages machinery tailored to narrow operations. At the economy level, a larger division of labor creates interdependence: productivity rises because workers rely on extensive exchange networks. This makes transport, finance and market access productivity variables, not merely distribution systems. Modern models of firm specialization, supply chains and learning-by-doing formalize these ideas. The tradeoff is resilience: deep specialization can make production vulnerable to coordination failures and concentrated bottlenecks.",
    "econ-smith-market-size": "Smith's claim that the division of labor is limited by the extent of the market anticipates increasing-returns and economic-geography models. A larger market supports fixed costs of specialized occupations, machinery and intermediate suppliers that would not be viable with small demand. Better transport or trade access can therefore raise productivity by expanding feasible specialization, not merely by moving existing goods more cheaply. The relationship can be cumulative: larger markets support specialization, specialization raises productivity and lower costs can expand markets further. This is one reason urban agglomeration and trade integration can generate effects larger than simple tariff arithmetic suggests.",
    "econ-smith-public-works": "Smith assigned government roles in defense, justice and public works that are valuable to society but may not yield enough private revenue for individual provision. This is close to a modern public-goods and infrastructure argument, though his institutional language differs. Roads, bridges and education can expand market size and productive capability, complementing private exchange rather than replacing it. Smith also considered user charges where beneficiaries can be identified, showing a concern with financing and incentives. His position is therefore not a binary state-versus-market doctrine; it asks which functions private exchange can sustain and which require collective organization.",
    "econ-smith-mercantilism": "Smith's critique of mercantilism targeted the conflation of national wealth with money holdings and the political privileges granted to merchants through monopolies and trade restrictions. He argued that imports can raise real income by allowing consumers and producers to obtain goods at lower opportunity cost. He also suspected that producer interests were often more organized than consumer interests, anticipating public-choice themes. Smith was not an absolute free-trade dogmatist; he allowed exceptions involving defense and transitional concerns. The enduring analytical move is to evaluate trade by real resource costs and consumption possibilities, not by whether money flows into or out of the country.",
    "econ-smith-wages-profits-rents": "Classical distribution separates income into wages to labor, profits to capital and rents to land. Smith and Ricardo analyzed how these shares respond differently to accumulation, population and scarcity. Land rent is especially distinctive because it can rise from location or scarcity without corresponding new production effort. Profits provide a return to advancing capital and bearing commercial uncertainty, while wages depend on labor demand and subsistence institutions. Modern national accounts use different categories, but the factor-income perspective remains useful for understanding how growth is distributed. The classical question is not only how much the economy produces, but who receives the incremental surplus.",
    "econ-entrepreneur-schumpeter": "The Schumpeterian entrepreneur is an innovator who introduces new combinations rather than merely managing an existing firm. Innovation can mean a new product, production method, market, input source or organizational form. Because successful innovation disrupts established routines, entrepreneurship is disequilibrating in the short run even though it drives long-run growth. Finance is central because entrepreneurs need command over resources before the innovation generates cash flow. Later Schumpeter placed more weight on large corporate research organizations, so his own work supports both startup-led and incumbent-led innovation mechanisms.",
    "econ-innovation-rents": "Innovation rents are temporary returns above competitive normal profit earned by a successful innovator. These rents provide incentives to incur uncertain research, development and commercialization costs. Patents deliberately create some exclusivity to protect them, while secrecy, lead time and complementary assets can do the same without formal intellectual property. The policy problem is duration and scope: too little appropriability can underprovide innovation, but excessively durable market power reduces diffusion and follow-on competition. Optimal innovation policy therefore balances dynamic incentives against static markup losses and may use prizes, subsidies or public research where patents are poorly targeted.",
    "econ-business-cycles-schumpeter": "Schumpeter linked business fluctuations to clusters of innovations financed by credit. Major innovations can trigger waves of investment, entry and restructuring, followed by periods in which the economy absorbs new capacity and unsuccessful projects are liquidated. This differs from purely monetary or demand-driven cycle theories because the disturbance originates in technological and organizational change. Modern evidence supports uneven diffusion of general-purpose technologies, but standard macroeconomics does not treat innovation clustering as a complete theory of all recessions. The useful insight is that growth and cycles can interact: rapid structural change can generate both booms and painful reallocation.",
    "econ-capitalism-success": "Schumpeter's paradoxical prediction was that capitalism could undermine its own social foundations through success. Large firms bureaucratize innovation, intellectual classes criticize the system, and the family and property structures that historically supported entrepreneurship may weaken. This is a sociological and political argument rather than a formal economic law. Its continuing relevance lies in the idea that institutions can alter the constituencies that sustain them. Economic systems therefore cannot be evaluated only by productive efficiency; legitimacy, organizational structure and political coalitions feed back into long-run institutional stability.",
    "econ-innovation-diffusion": "The economic value of an invention depends on adoption. Firms need complementary capital, worker skills, organizational change and infrastructure before a new technology affects measured productivity. Diffusion can be slow because adopters face uncertainty, financing constraints and coordination problems. Network effects can accelerate adoption after a threshold, while standards battles can delay it. This helps explain the productivity paradox around new general-purpose technologies: large investment can occur before aggregate productivity visibly rises. Policy that subsidizes invention without addressing diffusion bottlenecks may therefore leave much of the potential social return unrealized.",
    "econ-general-purpose-tech": "A general-purpose technology has broad applicability, continual improvement and strong complementarities with downstream innovation. Steam power, electricity, computing and potentially artificial intelligence fit the framework to varying degrees. Productivity gains can arrive slowly because firms must redesign processes, accumulate complementary capital and develop new organizational forms. During that transition measured productivity can even look weak despite heavy investment. General-purpose technologies also create spillovers, so private innovators may capture only part of the social return. This provides a rationale for public research, standards and infrastructure when coordination failures or knowledge externalities are large.",
    "econ-incumbent-disruption": "Incumbents often possess capital, brands and distribution advantages, yet those same assets can create resistance to technologies that cannibalize existing profits. Organizational routines, internal incentives and customer expectations can make a disruptive project unattractive inside a successful firm even when it is socially valuable. Entrants with fewer legacy assets may therefore pursue the new technology more aggressively. This is not inevitable: incumbents can acquire startups, create autonomous divisions or leverage complementary assets to dominate the new market. The empirical question is how incentives and capabilities interact, not whether incumbents are always slow.",
    "econ-dynamic-competition": "Dynamic competition evaluates rivalry through innovation, entry and technological replacement over time rather than only through current prices. A firm with a large market share can still face strong discipline if entrants can overturn its position through superior technology. Conversely, high profits can become persistent when incumbents control data, standards, distribution or acquisitions that block future challengers. Antitrust therefore faces a difficult counterfactual: would intervention protect the innovation process or weaken incentives to invest? Static price-cost margins remain informative, but they are not sufficient when the main competitive variable is technological trajectory.",
    "econ-north-institutions": "North defined institutions as the rules of the game and organizations as the players operating within them. Institutions reduce uncertainty by structuring interaction, but they can be inefficient and persistent because organizations adapt to the existing incentive structure and then invest in preserving it. North's later work emphasized beliefs, informal norms and path dependence, not only formal property rights. Economic change is therefore incremental and historically conditioned. This framework helps explain why transplanting a formal law from one country to another can produce different outcomes when enforcement capacity and informal norms differ.",
    "econ-path-dependence": "Path dependence means earlier events alter the payoff structure of later choices, so history affects which equilibrium is reached. Increasing returns, learning, network effects and institutional complementarities can lock in arrangements even when alternatives later appear superior. The concept is stronger than saying history matters: there must be a mechanism by which sequence changes future costs or benefits. Not every persistent institution is inefficient lock-in; persistence may reflect ongoing advantages. Empirical work therefore tries to identify historical shocks that shifted one path relative to another and trace the reinforcing mechanism forward.",
    "econ-credible-commitment": "A credible commitment problem arises when a promise that is optimal ex ante becomes tempting to break ex post. Governments may promise not to expropriate investors, central banks may promise low inflation, and borrowers may promise prudent behavior after receiving funds. If private agents anticipate reversal, they change behavior before the promise is tested, reducing investment or raising risk premia. Institutions such as independent courts, central-bank independence, collateral and reputation can make reversal costly. Credibility is therefore an equilibrium property of incentives, not merely a verbal assurance.",
    "econ-transaction-cost-institutions": "Institutions can be understood as technologies for reducing transaction costs. Standardized contracts lower bargaining costs, registries reduce uncertainty over ownership, courts lower expected enforcement costs, and reputation systems substitute for some formal verification. But institutions themselves consume resources and can create rigidities. A highly formal legal process may protect rights yet be too expensive for small transactions. Comparative institutional analysis therefore asks which mix of formal and informal enforcement minimizes total coordination costs in a particular setting. The optimal arrangement can change as market scale, technology and social networks evolve.",
    "econ-property-rights-growth": "Secure expected control over future returns increases the incentive to make investments whose payoff is delayed or relationship-specific. If assets can be arbitrarily seized, owners rationally shorten horizons, keep capital mobile or underinvest in maintenance. But stronger rights are not automatically growth-enhancing when they protect monopoly privileges or block reallocation. The relevant distinction is secure rights to productive claims versus rents created by exclusion. Land titling, for example, can improve collateral and investment in some settings but have small effects where informal rights are already secure or credit markets remain constrained.",
    "econ-informal-institutions": "Informal institutions include norms, trust, customs, reputational sanctions and unwritten expectations. They can support exchange where courts are weak by making opportunism costly within repeated networks. The same mechanisms can also exclude outsiders or reinforce inefficient conventions. Formal reforms sometimes fail because they conflict with informal practices, while successful formal institutions often build on existing norms. This is why institutional change is not equivalent to passing a law. Enforcement expectations and social legitimacy determine whether written rules alter behavior.",
    "econ-institutional-persistence": "Institutions persist because they create complementary investments, expectations and political beneficiaries. Firms learn to operate under existing regulations, workers acquire institution-specific skills, and groups receiving rents organize to defend the arrangement. Changing one rule can therefore be costly unless complementary rules change too. Persistence need not imply inefficiency: stable institutions can reduce uncertainty and support long-term planning. The analytical problem is distinguishing productive commitment from lock-in. Historical shocks are often valuable empirically because they reveal whether persistence reflects increasing returns or continuing fundamentals.",
    "econ-inclusive-extractive": "The inclusive-versus-extractive framework argues that broad political participation and secure economic opportunities support innovation and investment, while institutions that concentrate power facilitate rent extraction. Its strength is the emphasis on political foundations of economic incentives and the possibility that elites block technologies threatening their position. Its weakness is that the categories can become broad and difficult to measure independently of outcomes. Countries also combine inclusive features in some domains with extractive ones in others. The framework is therefore most useful when translated into specific mechanisms such as property security, entry barriers, political accountability or elite control over rents.",
    "econ-prospect-theory-econ": "Prospect theory evaluates outcomes relative to a reference point rather than final wealth. Its value function is typically concave over gains, convex over losses and steeper for losses, while probability weighting overweights some small probabilities and underweights some moderate or large ones. This can generate behavior inconsistent with expected utility, including simultaneous demand for insurance and lottery-like payoffs. Cumulative prospect theory improves treatment of multiple outcomes. Applications in finance and labor economics depend heavily on how reference points are formed, which remains one of the main empirical challenges.",
    "econ-mental-accounting-econ": "Mental accounting describes how people partition wealth into separate psychological accounts instead of treating every euro as perfectly fungible. A household may simultaneously carry expensive credit-card debt and preserve a low-yield savings account because the accounts have different labels and goals. This can be interpreted as a bias, but accounts can also be useful self-control devices that reduce decision complexity. In firms, budget silos can generate similar nonfungibility. The economic question is whether partitioning improves behavior by creating commitment or creates avoidable arbitrage losses.",
    "econ-hyperbolic-econ": "Hyperbolic discounting produces declining discount rates over the horizon, so preferences between two future rewards can reverse as both rewards become imminent. Quasi-hyperbolic beta-delta models capture this with a one-time present-bias factor plus ordinary exponential discounting thereafter. The resulting dynamic inconsistency explains procrastination and demand for commitment devices. Sophisticated agents anticipate future self-control problems and may lock in saving or deadlines; naive agents repeatedly postpone. Welfare analysis is difficult because different temporal selves disagree, forcing the analyst to decide which preference representation should count.",
    "econ-behavioral-public-policy": "Behavioral public policy uses empirical regularities such as inertia, limited attention and framing to redesign taxes, disclosures, defaults and administrative processes. The strongest applications target a documented friction and measure downstream welfare-relevant outcomes, not only immediate choice changes. Interventions can fail when effects decay, when people adapt or when firms strategically respond. There is also a normative problem: if preferences are unstable, policymakers need a defensible criterion for deciding which choice is better for the individual. Transparent opt-out, heterogeneity analysis and comparison with price-based tools are therefore essential.",
    "econ-kahneman-tversky": "Kahneman and Tversky showed that judgment under uncertainty systematically departs from simple statistical and expected-utility benchmarks. Heuristics such as representativeness and availability can produce predictable errors, while prospect theory formalized reference dependence and nonlinear probability weighting. Their work shifted economics from treating deviations as random noise toward modeling them as structured behavior. Later research has refined effect sizes and identified boundary conditions, but the methodological legacy remains: benchmark models should be tested against actual decisions, and alternative models must make discriminating predictions rather than rely on vague claims about irrationality.",
    "econ-thaler": "Thaler helped integrate psychological evidence into economic models through concepts such as mental accounting, the endowment effect, self-control problems and choice architecture. His work often preserves the logic of incentives while adding realistic frictions in attention and preference. In retirement saving, for example, automatic enrollment and escalation exploit inertia and present bias to change outcomes without banning alternatives. Thaler also emphasized seemingly small anomalies as clues that the benchmark model is missing a systematic mechanism. The broader contribution is to make behavioral economics cumulative and institutionally applicable rather than a catalog of isolated biases.",
    "econ-bounded-self-control": "Self-control models distinguish long-run plans from short-run temptations. A present-biased consumer may prefer today to save next month but, when next month arrives, prefer immediate consumption again. This creates demand for commitment devices such as automatic transfers, withdrawal penalties or deadlines. Firms can either help by selling commitment or exploit the bias through teaser rates and complex cancellation. Welfare analysis depends on whether consumers are sophisticated about their future behavior. If they are naive, observed market choices can systematically diverge from their own earlier plans.",
    "econ-fairness-preferences": "Experimental evidence from ultimatum, dictator and public-goods games shows that many people care about reciprocity, inequality or intentions in addition to own monetary payoff. Models such as inequity aversion and reciprocity preferences formalize these motives and can explain wage rigidity, gift exchange and cooperation. Behavior is strongly context-dependent, so fairness should not be treated as a fixed universal parameter. Market experience and anonymity can alter responses. The economic significance is that contracts and institutions can affect outcomes by changing perceived intentions and norms, not only by changing material incentives."
  }
};
